{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath '.agents/skills/labview-automation/SKILL.md' -Encoding UTF8\"","aggregated_output":"---\r\nname: labview-automation\r\ndescription: Automate LabVIEW programmatically - edit block diagrams with VI Scripting, and drive the LabVIEW GUI reliably when scripting cannot reach. Use whenever a task involves creating, editing, wiring or inspecting a LabVIEW .vi; enabling loop parallelism; dropping subVIs; adding structures or constants; decoding VI Scripting errors (1057/1059); or clicking anything inside the LabVIEW editor. Also use when choosing a VI Scripting library, or when GUI automation of LabVIEW is misclicking, hitting the wrong window, or failing to wire terminals.\r\n---\r\n\r\n# LabVIEW Automation\r\n\r\n**PORTABLE SKILL — copy this whole directory into any LabVIEW project.** Nothing here is\r\nproject-specific. **Verified on:** LabVIEW 2026 (26.3.1f1, Q3), Windows 10, 1920×1080, single\r\ndisplay, VI Scripting enabled — re-verify colours/pixel offsets on any other setup.\r\n\r\nThis file is the INDEX. The technique lives one layer down — open the reference for the topic you\r\nare actually doing, in full:\r\n\r\n| you are about to… | read |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| create/wire/inspect diagram objects by script; pick a library; follow the wiring protocol; design ops | [references/vi-scripting.md](references/vi-scripting.md) |\r\n| drive LabVIEW from Python/COM: run VIs, set controls, save, diagnose hangs/silent failures | [references/com-driving.md](references/com-driving.md) |\r\n| click anything in the editor: Quick Drop, class picker, wiring by hand, resize, menus, screenshots | [references/gui-recipes.md](references/gui-recipes.md) |\r\n| read `.vi` files offline; find where NI installs things; persist IMAQ images bit-exactly | [references/files-and-formats.md](references/files-and-formats.md) |\r\n\r\n## Rule 0 — prefer code over clicking, always\r\n\r\n1. **VI Scripting** (enable in Tools ▸ Options ▸ VI Server) via an installed library — do not\r\n   hand-build primitives. Measured: a task costing **31–40 GUI tool calls is ONE scripting call**.\r\n2. Only two operations have NO scripting path (externally verified): **placing a primitive outside\r\n   `New VI Object`'s style ring** and **growing a node's terminal count**. Everything else that\r\n   looks unscriptable has turned out scriptable — search before declaring a third.\r\n3. A click into a DELIVERABLE repeats on every rebuild; a click into a TOOL is paid once. When a\r\n   scripted path exists in principle, build the tool — \"only a few clicks left\" is how the tool\r\n   never gets built.\r\n\r\n## The five laws that bite hardest (details in the references)\r\n\r\n- **Verify by effect, never by return code or wire count.** Silent decline is the normal failure\r\n  mode; a successful BRANCH adds no Wire object; `ExecState` 0→1 is the unforgeable signal.\r\n- **Names are the API and they are exact bytes** — labels contain real newlines and trailing\r\n  spaces. Keep and consult the project's verified-name registry before every wiring call.\r\n- **Open the panel before editing, save before closing, and never cold-load a broken-saved VI\r\n  headless** (recompile spin). After **error 2** (memory full): restart FIRST — a Ctrl+S in that\r\n  state can write a stale file.\r\n- **Menus, wiring mode, palettes and Context Help leave LabVIEW modal — every later COM call hangs\r\n  until `Esc`.** A hang with no dialog is usually this, not a wedge.\r\n- **Trust the foreground TITLE PIXELS, not the focus call's return** — keystrokes have landed in\r\n  other applications. Verify, then type.\r\n\r\n## Safety\r\n\r\n- **Never modify an original `.vi`.** Work on copies. A `*` in the title is not a disk change —\r\n  only Save is. If an original goes dirty: close WITHOUT saving, verify checksum (`-Action md5`).\r\n  Answer **Don't Save** to every save prompt on an original, and audit saved-version bytes after\r\n  sessions that opened originals (`19 00 80 00` = LV2019, `26 00 80 00` = LV2026 — a newer save is\r\n  irreversible).\r\n- **Copy the whole dependency folder** before opening a VI with subVIs (else \"Find the VI Named X\"\r\n  cascades).\r\n- **One execution path at a time** — never two automations, or automation + human, on one LabVIEW.\r\n- On a machine wired to real hardware: confirm before driving anything, and know which instruments\r\n  must never be actuated.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$paths = @('\"'AGENTS.md','CLAUDE.md','tools/bench/cards/task_90-6.json','tools/bench/cards/result_90-5.json','tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3b.log','tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3b_r2.log','tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.py','tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.log','tools/bench/t0_sites_s1.json','archive/peer/2026-09-26-c90-t0step3-movewire.md'); foreach (\"'$p in $paths) { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $p) { Write-Output ('\"'FILE: ' + \"'$p); $n = 0; Get-Content -LiteralPath $p -Encoding UTF8 | ForEach-Object { $n++; '\"'{0}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_ } } }'","aggregated_output":"FILE: AGENTS.md\r\n1: ---\r\n2: type: rules\r\n3: status: current\r\n4: date: 2026-09-15\r\n5: tags: [rules]\r\n6: ---\r\n7: \r\n8: # Standing rules — read before doing anything\r\n9: \r\n10: ## 1. Never modify an original file. Ever.\r\n11: \r\n12: Applies to **every** pre-existing `.vi` this project touches: the reference VI\r\n13: `Min_Track N beads 4.5_KimLabMTroom_3StateClamping.vi`, everything in `zz_LabView VI\\background\r\n14: VIs\\`, and any original opened for inspection. **Copies are always fine** — new/derived work goes in\r\n15: `C:\\Program Files\\National Instruments\\LabVIEW 2026\\user.lib\\claudeDev`, never saved over an\r\n16: original, never into this project folder.\r\n17: \r\n18: A `*` in a LabVIEW title bar does **not** mean the disk file changed — only an explicit Save does.\r\n19: If an original ends up dirty: close **without saving**, verify the on-disk checksum, continue.\r\n20: Answer **Don't Save** to every save prompt naming an original (merely *opening* a hierarchy in a\r\n21: newer LabVIEW relinks subVIs, dirties them, and one \"Save\" on exit rewrites the whole hierarchy in\r\n22: the newer format — irreversibly). After any session that opened originals, audit saved-version\r\n23: bytes offline: `19 00 80 00` = LV2019, `26 00 80 00` = LV2026.\r\n24: \r\n25: ## 1a. Never change the original's COMPUTATION — this is a behaviour-preserving refactor\r\n26: \r\n27: **User, 2026-08-30: \"원본의 연산방법 자체를 바꾸면 안돼. 이건 꼭 명심하고.\"** Only *scheduling* may\r\n28: change (shift registers → auto-indexed tunnels, parallel instances). The per-bead maths, the\r\n29: parameters that reach it, and the numbers that come out must be identical.\r\n30: \r\n31: - A change of decomposition can be a change of computation — any kernel substitution must be\r\n32:   justified as equivalent or checked numerically, never assumed.\r\n33: - Parameters must arrive by the same route with the same values; falling back to a default is a\r\n34:   computation change in disguise. Whole-array parameters must cross loop borders **non-indexed**.\r\n35: - **Acceptance is numeric**: same inputs → same X/Y/Z through old and new paths. `ExecState == 1`\r\n36:   says nothing about equivalence.\r\n37: - When a step cannot be shown computation-preserving, **stop and ask the user**.\r\n38: \r\n39: ## 1b. Hardware permission is a function of the RIG STATE — and the ASI is not a special case\r\n40: \r\n41: **RE-ISSUED 2026-09-16 by the user**, replacing every earlier hardware rule:\r\n42: *\"모터 접근 및 카메라 접근을 '리그 분해 / 리그 조립 / 실험중' 상태에 따라 다르게 두는게 맞는듯. 리그 분해 →\r\n43: 모든 모터 및 카메라 접근 허용. 리그 조립 → 카메라 접근만 허용. 실험중 → 모든 모터 및 카메라 접근 불가.\r\n44: 이는 Piezo stage인 ASI 컨트롤러를 포함하는 내용 (ASI와 다른 모터를 구분하여 권한 두지 말것).\"*\r\n45: \r\n46: | rig state | motors (PI translation · rotor · magnet) | **ASI piezo stage** | camera |\r\n47: |---|---|---|---|\r\n48: | **분해 — disassembled** | ✅ allowed | ✅ **allowed, exactly like any other motor** | ✅ allowed |\r\n49: | **조립 — assembled** | ⚠️ **ONLY through `tools/motor_gate.py`, inside the safe-motion envelope** | ⚠️ same gateway, same envelope | ✅ allowed |\r\n50: | **실험중 — experiment running** | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |\r\n51: \r\n52: - **The 조립 row was ❌ ❌ until the user amended it on 2026-09-17 evening** (*\"조립 상태에서도 이 범위 안이면 모터\r\n53:   허용함\"*, after *\"실험 1차로 끝났는데, 리그는 유지되는 중\"*). The later statement NARROWS rather than widens: motors\r\n54:   and the ASI are reachable while assembled **only** through the single gateway `tools/motor_gate.py` and only\r\n55:   inside the safe-motion envelope (PI magnet 0–39 mm · ASI x/y inside the `SL`/`SU` limits the user set AT THE RIG on\r\n56:   2026-09-18 15:2x — live position **±2 mm**, never homed · ASI z free). ⚠️ **The numbers live in `STATUS.md`'s\r\n57:   hardware banner and in `tools/bench/motor_limits.json`; never restate them here.** This line said \"≤ ~1 mm from the\r\n58:   anchor\" until 2026-09-19, when `doc_ingest` measured it against the banner's absolute `SL`/`SU` values (a 4.0 mm-wide\r\n59:   window in each axis) and the cycle-41 judgement session resolved it the same way rule 1b was resolved on 2026-09-18:\r\n60:   the later user statement wins and STATUS was the one that was right,\r\n61:   **conditional on that envelope being really enforced as refusing code**. The envelope says what is SAFE, not when\r\n62:   motors are allowed; the rig state still decides that. Numbers and the gate's live-move evidence:\r\n63:   `STATUS.md` hardware banner. Resolved 2026-09-18 by the cycle-18 judgement session after `doc_ingest` reported\r\n64:   this row contradicting STATUS.md — later user statement wins, and STATUS was the one that was right.\r\n65: - **Never give the ASI its own rule again.** The old carve-out (\"the one instrument that can physically break the\r\n66:   rig\", the 2026-09-13 permission conditioned on the piezo being *detached*) is **retired**. Permission is decided\r\n67:   by the rig's state, not by the instrument's identity — when the rig is apart there is nothing to collide with.\r\n68: - **CONTROLLER LIMITS ARE SET AT EVERY CYCLE START AND RELEASED AT EVERY CYCLE END, BY THE RUNNER, EACH VERIFIED BY READBACK** (user, 2026-09-23: \"훅으로 묶어서 매 사이클마다 시작할때는 묶고, 종료시에는 풀고. 그 다음 각 싸이클 시작 및 종료 시점마다 제대로 리밋 셋팅 되어있는지 확인하도록\"). `cycle_runner.py` `motor_limits_hook` runs `motor_gate.py --session start` before and `--session end` after every cycle; a start that does not verify stops the runner before the cycle, an end that does not verify stops it after and writes STATUS. In 실험중/unknown state nothing is sent and the skip is logged. **SESSION START = A REAL REFERENCE MOVE FIRST, THEN A COMMANDED-vs-READBACK CHECK, RETRIED UP TO 3× (user, 2026-09-23 after the near-miss: \"반드시 싸이클 시작할 때는 PI 모터 원점 복귀 반드시 시킨 후에 실제 좌표와 아웃풋 좌표 비교하는거 반드시 만들어\" · \"원점 복귀하여 복원 시키고 사이클 시작한다 … 여러 차례 복원 시도해도 문제가 생긴다면 그 때는 사이클 종료\"):** `motor_send_pi.ps1` limits-set now sends `SVO 1 1`, `RON 1 1`, `FNL 1` (0 = negative limit switch), requires `FRF? 1` and `POS 0`, then moves 2 mm and back and compares readback to the command within 0.05 mm; `motor_gate.session_start` retries the whole PI step `REF_ATTEMPTS`=3 times and refuses only after all fail, which is when the runner does not start the cycle. The old \"reference restore\" (`RON 1 0` + `POS 1 <current>`) is GONE: after a controller power-cycle the counter reads 0 wherever the stage physically is, and declaring that zero drove the magnet into the hard limit on 2026-09-23 (`tools/bench/pi_testmove_20260923e.log`, ERR 216). Why: `--session start` was run once on 2026-09-18 and `--session end` never, so the limits sat on the controllers for five days and had to be released by hand before an experiment (`tools/bench/motor_session_end_20260923.log`).\r\n69: - **MOTOR GRANT 2026-09-24 (user: \"당분간 내가 말하기 전까지는 모터 접속 허용함. 다만 원점 확인 및 모터 리밋, 두 가지는 꼭 확인 필요\"):** while it stands, motors may be driven by the gate and by a RUNNING MAIN VI with the rig assembled — a newly assembled main VI's acceptance test IS a real run (\"이제 메인 vi 조립시에는 실제 작동시켜야 할테니 그대로 해볼 것\"); recorded-frame replay is used only when a real run cannot answer. The two checks are never skipped (session-start reference + verify, limits set/released with readback). **EVERY CYCLE ENDS WITH LabVIEW CLOSED AND VERIFIED GONE** (\"사이클 종료하고서는 제대로 LabVIEW 끄는것 잊지 말것, 특히 카메라가 계속 Acquisition 하면 기계에 좋지 않으니\") — `cycle_runner.py` end hook, mechanical. The grant ends only when the user says so.\r\n70: - **Only the user announces a state change**, and the announcement is the boundary. Never infer a transition from\r\n71:   silence, from a quiet period, or from how long a session has run.\r\n72: - **Do not ask per incident** inside a state the user has already declared (*\"Don't need to hastle around me\r\n73:   before I tell you.\"*). Re-requesting permission each session is the error.\r\n74: - While **disassembled**, hardware measurement is expected work — true VISA/serial latency, the per-frame cost of\r\n75:   motor reading, the rotor negative-angle check on `SetCommand_signed.vi`. It is cheapest now and expensive after\r\n76:   reassembly, so it is not deferred to \"a supervised session\".\r\n77: - **The rotor is SIGNED — the new VI uses `SetCommand_signed.vi` and follows the hardware number, sign included**\r\n78:   (user, 2026-09-16: *\"로터는 본래 부호 인식이 가능했으나 랩뷰 시리얼 통신에서 부호 인식이 안되는 문제가 있었음.\r\n79:   이제는 해결 방법을 찾은 것 같으니 부호를 포함하여 하드웨어 숫자 그대로 따라가야함.\"*). The unsigned\r\n80:   `SetCommand.vi` was a workaround for a serial-path defect, not the rotor's nature. \"해결 방법을 찾은 것 **같으니**\"\r\n81:   — the sign round-trip on the real link is still to be measured while disassembled, not assumed.\r\n82: - Superseded and not to be reinstated from an old summary: the 2026-08-27 blanket motor ban\r\n83:   (*\"우선 모터 가동은 절대 하지 않는 선에서 계속 처리해\"*) and the piezo-detached condition.\r\n84: \r\n85: The realistic hazard remains a VI run as a side effect: LabVIEW's broken-arrow and Run buttons are the same\r\n86: pixels — check the arrow state or use menus before clicking that toolbar region on any VI that can reach an\r\n87: instrument.\r\n88: \r\n89: ## 1c. No serial on the frame path — the user's data-quality constraint (2026-09-16)\r\n90: \r\n91: *\"I don't want to have even a single frame loss coming from the motor communication if possible. And it is so\r\n92: clear that serial communication through VISA can somehow stall the loop, and cause unwanted frame stop.\"* And the\r\n93: fact behind it: the sample stage **drifts on its own** (thermal drift, the sample-holder pin shifting, causes the\r\n94: user cannot name), so **focus re-adjustment is continuous, not rare** — \"I can't tell how frequent it is, but it\r\n95: is not trivial.\"\r\n96: \r\n97: So the constraint is structural, not statistical: **no VISA/serial call may sit anywhere on the frame acquisition\r\n98: path.** A mechanism that *can* stall the frame loop is disqualified even if it usually does not. Serial lives in\r\n99: its own loop, owns its VISA session exclusively, and reaches the frame path only through a non-blocking handoff.\r\n100: Any argument of the form \"the serial branch is conditional, so it is cheap on average\" is refuted at the premise.\r\n101: \r\n102: ## 1c''. Loop-to-loop CONTROL signals travel by LOCAL VARIABLE (latest value), never by queue; the focus loop runs on its own clock (user, 2026-09-25)\r\n103: \r\n104: *\"큐를 넣어버린다면 두 루프 사이에 상관관계가 생겨버린다 … 그런 리스크를 감당할 필요가 있는지 모르겠음. 그냥 Boolean 값 및\r\n105: 타겟 값을 local variable로 전달하는게 더 좋지 않을지? ASI autofocus 루프는 애초에 frame acquisition 루프와 동시에 돌\r\n106: 필요가 없을듯.\"* A queue couples producer and consumer (backlog, timeouts, shutdown order); a local variable does not —\r\n107: the writer never waits and the reader sees the latest value. So: **control / trigger signals between loops (focus\r\n108: requests, setpoints, enable flags, counters) are locals; queues are for lossless DATA streams only** (the file writer's\r\n109: results FIFO, master plan 1.7). **Never detect an EDGE on a polled boolean** (that is what produced STATUS OPEN 58's\r\n110: three autofocus limits); publish a value the reader can compare (a counter, a position).\r\n111: \r\n112: **Autofocus — the user's account (2026-09-25) and what the code MEASURES:** the user remembers a distance-threshold\r\n113: trigger on the **first-clicked reference bead**; the offline read of Case `#10407` (`docs/autofocus-case-10407.md`,\r\n114: card chat-F1) shows NO threshold: every firing (every 25 frames, `camera-acquisition-facts.md:255-270`) moves the axis\r\n115: by `clamp(idx_bead0 − slices/2 + 'Focus Deviation from the Center', ±0.2)` — a proportional correction with an\r\n116: additive setpoint OFFSET, on bead index 0; `Focus Step (F1)` is not read there; the `In Range?` node is unwired.\r\n117: **Rule 1a decides: the redesign copies the CODE, not the memory.** A threshold trigger would be a new feature and\r\n118: needs the user's separate decision. The user does not run with `Frame rate` = 1. DESIGN IN FORCE for the focus loop\r\n119: (after M3, `docs/connectivity-map-plan.md` Pre-decided 190 — renumbered 2026-09-25 from 148, which clashed with `d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md`): frame loop publishes the reference bead's z and the\r\n120: auto-reset counter as locals; the focus loop runs on its own time cadence, reads them, applies the original's\r\n121: correction arithmetic (offset, clamp ±0.2, bead index 0) under `Auto-Focus` / `Limit of Auto-Focus`, and moves — no\r\n122: schedule boolean in the frame loop, no edge detection, no queue. Scheduling changes; the arithmetic does not (rule 1a).\r\n123: Mechanical: `tools/stage_prerun.py` refuses a stage plan that creates a queue primitive for a control signal or\r\n124: wires a boolean into a shift-register edge detector across loops (`control_path_lint`).\r\n125: \r\n126: ## 1c'. Test runs are UNATTENDED until the rig is reassembled — the harness clicks the beads itself (user, 2026-09-17)\r\n127: \r\n128: The main VI runs as: panel parameters → device configure → **a while loop that waits for mouse clicks on the live\r\n129: image (first = reference bead, rest = magnetic beads)** → **a button** that ends it and runs bead-profile\r\n130: calibration → the experiment loop. Nobody present ⇒ it stalls in the picking loop. The user's requirement:\r\n131: *\"리그 조립 전까지는 내가 없는 환경에서 돌리기를 기대함 … 몇십번이고 돌려야 할테니 … 밤 시간동안 하네스 루프\r\n132: 돌리며 개발 하기를 기대함.\"* Their choice of mechanism, **option 1**: *\"직접 마우스 움직여서 디스플레이 상에 클릭\r\n133: 이후 버튼 누르기 → 저장 위치 및 저장 이름 쓰기\"* — i.e. scripted GUI clicks on the image display, the done\r\n134: button, and the save-path entry, through `tools/lv_gui.ps1` with `-Exception Approved -Evidence \"user 2026-09-17\r\n135: bead-pick option 1\"`. A `.cal`-loading substitute VI (option 2) was **rejected**. *\"Bead가 없는 상황에서 트래킹\r\n136: 에러가 분명히 생기겠으나, 디바이스 통신 및 프레임 체크 용도로는 무리 없을 듯\"* — garbage tracking output in a\r\n137: no-bead run is not a failure. **GPU path first; CPU afterwards** (re-affirmed the same day).\r\n138: \r\n139: ## 1d. The main VI is in scope — read-only, and read it when meaning requires it\r\n140: \r\n141: (User, 2026-08-30: examine the main VI when the situation calls for it; effort belongs on\r\n142: *semantic* analysis.) Never modify, never save; prefer **headless COM reads** over editor windows.\r\n143: **Read nested structures properly** — a flat strings dump once made calibration-cluster *fields*\r\n144: look like ignorable stray controls.\r\n145: \r\n146: ## 2. LabVIEW is shared with real experiments — be interruptible at any point\r\n147: \r\n148: The user may forcibly stop a session at any moment to reclaim LabVIEW. Keep `STATUS.md`'s state\r\n149: current **during** work — after each real milestone, not just at session end. Treat \"the user might\r\n150: vanish right now\" as the normal case.\r\n151: \r\n152: ### 2b. The user's messages during work are interrupts — answer first, then continue\r\n153: \r\n154: (User, 2026-08-27; re-issued 2026-08-28 after a compaction let it fade; re-issued 2026-09-04\r\n155: \"전체 세션을 돌더라도 항상 응답 즉각적으로 할 수 있게 규칙 잡지 않았었나?\".) A user message that\r\n156: arrives mid-turn is answered in the **very next reply, first line, before any tool call** — a\r\n157: progress line (\"지금 ~ 확인 중\") is not an answer, and a yes/no question gets its yes/no in the first\r\n158: sentence, explanation after. Stop or redirect the running work if that is what the message implies.\r\n159: Background anything slow; prefer short checkable steps to long chains. Re-read this whenever the\r\n160: session is summarized.\r\n161: \r\n162: ## 2c. Once a cycle is turning, RUN IT TO THE END — do not pause it to ask (user, 2026-09-16)\r\n163: \r\n164: *\"한 번 루프 돌기 시작했을 때, 꼭 필요하지 않다면 중간에 멈추지 않고 끝까지 루프 계속 진행했으면 좋겠음.\r\n165: 규약에도 갱신하면 좋을듯.\"* Said after a session stopped mid-cycle to put a genuine question to the user — a gate\r\n166: was blocking the build over a date-comparison bug — while other work in the same cycle was still runnable.\r\n167: \r\n168: **The default is: finish the cycle, then report once.** When something is uncertain mid-cycle: (1) do every part\r\n169: that does not depend on the answer, all of it; (2) for the part that does, **write the assumption down, proceed\r\n170: under it**, and flag it in the closing report so the user overturns one thing rather than unblocks ten; (3) stop\r\n171: only when proceeding under *any* assumption would be unsafe, destructive, or would make the work useless if wrong.\r\n172: \r\n173: This is about **where the waiting happens**. A question asked mid-cycle makes the user the scheduler; the same\r\n174: question in the closing report lets them answer whenever, with the finished work already in front of them. It does\r\n175: not weaken 2b — their messages arriving mid-work are still answered in the very next reply, first line. Claude\r\n176: stays responsive to the user; the user does not become responsible for keeping the cycle moving.\r\n177: \r\n178: ## 3. Autonomous progress protocol\r\n179: \r\n180: **Start/stop is explicit, never inferred** — the user says start (\"지금 진행해\") and stop (\"멈춰\");\r\n181: silence is NOT authorization. Maintain the lock block in `STATUS.md` (acquired ↔ released) so any\r\n182: future session can tell at a glance whether LabVIEW is safe to touch:\r\n183: \r\n184: ```yaml\r\n185: labview-lock:\r\n186:   status: released   # acquired | released\r\n187:   owner:\r\n188:   since:\r\n189:   purpose:\r\n190: ```\r\n191: \r\n192: - **GUI only where scripting is VERIFIED unreachable** (user, 2026-08-31: \"GUI 활용은 script로\r\n193:   도달 불가능한 경우에만\"). **Mechanically enforced**: `lv_gui.ps1` refuses state-changing actions\r\n194:   (click/rclick/dclick/drag/wire/keys) without `-Exception VerifiedImpossible|NegativeSearch|Approved`\r\n195:   plus `-Evidence <record>`, and logs every authorized one to `tools/gui_actions.log`. The\r\n196:   verified-impossible list (skill; currently 2 entries) names operation CLASSES — each concrete use\r\n197:   must also show no scriptable alternative construction exists. Cost/time is never a reason. Known\r\n198:   laundering patterns to refuse in review: a too-narrow \"negative search\", \"only opening it to\r\n199:   look\", and plan clauses like \"via GUI if needed\" (every GUI act must be individually enumerated\r\n200:   in the peer-reviewed plan). One execution path at a time.\r\n201: - **Failed batch ⇒ RECOVERY_LOCKED** (peer-reviewed design, 2026-08-31,\r\n202:   `archive/peer/...gui-rule-hole-analysis-review.md`): when a scripted batch hits an unpredicted\r\n203:   result, state-changing GUI is OFF; only diagnostics (screenshots, window lists, reporter reads,\r\n204:   Esc) are allowed. Produce a recovery packet (prediction, observation, evidence, competing\r\n205:   explanation, cheapest discriminating test), get a **successful** peer review (a timeout/quota\r\n206:   failure is not a review), then resume as a NEW batch — never by continuing the failed one\r\n207:   interactively. Recipes must state their prediction contracts (expected counts/states/tolerances)\r\n208:   before execution so \"failed prediction\" is machine-checkable.\r\n209: - **Save authority**: files under `user.lib\\claudeDev` may be saved autonomously. Never originals.\r\n210: - **Restart authority** (user, 2026-08-29: \"재시작 항상 오케이\"): restart/kill LabVIEW when it is\r\n211:   the right fix, no per-incident approval; save valuables to disk first.\r\n212: \r\n213: ### Reference hygiene — close every reference, and PROVE it by handle count (user, 2026-09-06)\r\n214: \r\n215: (\"이후 close reference 필수로 사용하도록 하고, 규칙에 확실히 규정할 것. 그리고 해당 규칙이 반영되는지\r\n216: 검증.\" — after LabVIEW turned sluggish/'응답 없음' at 32,480 handles following ~30 h of scripting.)\r\n217: \r\n218: 1. **Every VI Server reference is closed by whoever opened it**: op VIs wire `Close Reference`\r\n219:    on every ref they create (Open VI Reference, Traverse arrays, Get Outputs, New VI Object,\r\n220:    creator outputs); Python releases cached VI references on exit and never abandons a COM call\r\n221:    (a killed or hung client keeps its references alive inside LabVIEW).\r\n222: 2. **Measured, not assumed**: `tools/bench/handle_audit.py` attributes handle growth to operation\r\n223:    types; `bench_prep.py` reads LabVIEW's handle count before every batch and restarts the\r\n224:    instance above `HANDLE_LIMIT` (mechanical, standing restart permission). A new op is accepted\r\n225:    only if 20 runs leave the handle count flat (±100). **Baseline: this LabVIEW 2026 install holds\r\n226:    ~31,500 handles one minute after a fresh start** (measured 2026-09-06 04:16) — the first\r\n227:    \"32,480 = leak\" diagnosis was WRONG; judge growth relative to that baseline, never the absolute\r\n228:    number. Responsiveness itself is measured directly with `lv_gui.ps1 -Action ping`\r\n229:    (SendMessageTimeout latency per window: the metric behind '응답 없음').\r\n230: 3. Any batch that must kill a client (deadline) is followed by a handle read; a jump is logged as\r\n231:    a non-result and the instance restarted before the next batch.\r\n232: \r\n233: ### Usage-limit protocol (user, 2026-09-04)\r\n234: \r\n235: (\"만약 중간에 이용한도가 꽉 차면 (95% 초과할 것 같으면) 리뉴얼 시점보다 2분 이후로 일정 예약\r\n236: 걸어두고 하던 작업 이어가도록. 벤치 중간에 멈췄다면 해당 벤치는 다시 돌리도록.\")\r\n237: \r\n238: 1. **Detect.** Before each long unit of work (a benchmark cell, a batch build) check the usage\r\n239:    readout if one is available; a usage/rate-limit error is the reactive trigger. Never assume the\r\n240:    quota is fine because nothing complained.\r\n241: 2. **Schedule, don't wait.** At ~95 % or on a limit error, read the renewal time from the limit\r\n242:    message and schedule a wake-up at **renewal + 2 min** (session wake-up for ≤ 1 h, a scheduled\r\n243:    task otherwise). Write the resume point to `STATUS.md` first. Do not end the turn with \"resume\r\n244:    me later\" — the schedule is the resume.\r\n245: 3. **Rerun, don't resume.** A benchmark cell or build interrupted mid-run is invalid: after the\r\n246:    wake-up it is rerun **from the beginning** and the partial attempt is logged as a non-result\r\n247:    (which cell, when, why). Half-run numbers never enter a results table.\r\n248: 4. **Never wait on a user turn** for something a script can do (registering definitions, restarting\r\n249:    a tool, re-queuing a cell). If a route needs the user, say that in one sentence and build the\r\n250:    scripted route.\r\n251: \r\n252: ### Reports reach the user THROUGH THE CLAUDE SESSION — the user is remote (user, 2026-09-25)\r\n253: \r\n254: *\"보통은 내가 원격으로 러너 확인 및 지침을 줌. 그러니 클로드 세션으로 확인 필요함. 훅 경로 변경하도록.\"* A Windows toast,\r\n255: a local file or a session-only timer (CronCreate, ScheduleWakeup — both failed silently, 09-23 and 09-25) is not a\r\n256: report. Path in force: (1) `cycle_runner.py` writes `HEARTBEAT` at every cycle end and at RUNNER STOP\r\n257: (`tools/bench/heartbeat_latest.md`, mechanical, no model); (2) the app-level scheduled task `runner-cycle-report`\r\n258: (`~/.claude/scheduled-tasks/runner-cycle-report/SKILL.md`, every 30 min, survives session changes, runs while the app\r\n259: is open) turns new events into a Korean report (`tools/bench/reports/`), acks `report_gate`, and its completion\r\n260: notifies the main chat session, which relays the report verbatim and calls `PushNotification` (phone when mobile\r\n261: push is on); (3) `tools/hooks/report_gate.py` still blocks the chat's turn on unreported events when the user speaks\r\n262: first. Open user decisions (`tools/bench/decisions_pending.json`) are part of every report.\r\n263: \r\n264: ### Unattended runs: silence is not progress (2026-09-05) — now mechanical\r\n265: \r\n266: A log monitor only reports what a live process writes. Two mechanisms replace attention:\r\n267: (1) `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py` refuses any backgrounded LabVIEW command unless it runs through\r\n268: `py tools/bgrun.py --max-min N --log <file> -- <cmd>`, which kills the process tree at the deadline\r\n269: and always writes a final `BGRUN END|TIMEOUT` line; (2) gscript's `_run/_invoke` watchdog caps\r\n270: Run/Invoke calls; an attempt to route GetVIReference/SetControlValue through the same thread\r\n271: (`tools/gscript.py.guarded_attempt_20260905`) itself blocked in cross-apartment marshalling and was\r\n272: rolled back — the PROCESS-level deadline of bgrun is the guarantee, not per-call guards. (User, 2026-09-05: \"Timeout 검사에서 우회된 내용이 있는 것 같은데 규칙 개선\r\n273: 필요할듯\", after a background job hung 3 h 48 min.) Original prose rule kept below for the why.\r\n274: \r\n275: A log monitor only reports what a live process writes; a driver that dies writes nothing, and the\r\n276: session sat 2.5 h believing a benchmark was running. While anything unattended runs, schedule a\r\n277: heartbeat (ScheduleWakeup / cron, ≤ 30 min) that checks the process list AND the log's age, and\r\n278: treat \"no new line for longer than one cell\" as a failure to diagnose, not as waiting.\r\n279: \r\n280: ### Usage discipline — fewer turns, one cycle per session (user, 2026-09-14: \"턴수 줄이는것 해보고 주기적 컨텍스트 정리도 적용해보자\")\r\n281: \r\n282: The user found Fable usage burning \"말도 안되게 빠른\" after a 13-hour session. The burn is **turns × context**: every\r\n283: background notification is a full turn over the whole conversation. Standing rules:\r\n284: \r\n285: 1. **One LabVIEW batch = one runner = one notification.** Chain build → test → archive/INDEX bookkeeping into a single\r\n286:    Python runner under one `bgrun` (as `build_opqueue_all.py` does); never a build turn, then a test turn, then a doc\r\n287:    turn. Dispatch the mandatory peer review AND prepare the fix in the same turn; read logs with a targeted grep, not\r\n288:    whole files; never re-read what is already in context.\r\n289: 2. **Session = one cycle.** Start a session by reading STATUS.md and the cycle's plan document only; end it by writing\r\n290:    STATUS's \"Next\" line and stop. Do not carry a second cycle in the same session — start a fresh one (the layered docs\r\n291:    exist for exactly this cold start; a fresh session beats compacting a long one).\r\n292:    **ENFORCED BY A RUNNER — user decision 2026-09-17 (\"2번으로 가자. Opus max\"), after a 15-hour session:** cycles are\r\n293:    not started by a person and not continued in a chat. `tools/cycle_runner.py` loops: spawn a fresh `claude -p`\r\n294:    judgement session (**Opus, effort max** — not Fable; **AMENDED 2026-09-23 by the user: `claude-opus-5-5` pinned by id at effort HIGH** — the Artificial Analysis index shows high→max = +4 points at 3.3× cost (news.hada.io/topic?id=34142); hypothesis reviews max→high, priorart 5.5/medium, material 5.5/medium, log-reader 5.5/low — **re-set 14:xx to the table the user approved (\"오케이 테이블대로 반영하자\") after the measured fact that Opus 5.5's effort scale differs: its medium scores as Opus 5's max on the Artificial Analysis index and is its default (platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort); judgement = medium**; the alias `opus` resolved to `claude-opus-5` and is no longer used, so a later alias change cannot silently move the comparison baseline; needs Claude Code ≥ 2.1.280) that reads STATUS.md + the current plan only, runs ONE cycle\r\n295:    (delegate → decide → retrospective → STATUS NEXT), exits; the runner checks the exit and NEXT and spawns the next.\r\n296:    Runner stop conditions: a `STOP` marker in STATUS.md (written by the user), the same failure two cycles running,\r\n297:    the usage-limit rule (renewal + 2 min). `tools/hooks/guard_session.py` (PreToolUse Agent) refuses material\r\n298:    dispatches after the retrospective has run in that session and above 8 dispatches per session. **The interactive\r\n299:    chat is for talking with the user only** — it answers from STATUS and the latest retrospective and redirects the\r\n300:    runner by editing STATUS NEXT/STOP; it never runs a cycle itself. Every cycle plan carries a `## Pre-decided`\r\n301:    section (decisions material sessions apply without asking; `doc_lint` warns when it is missing).\r\n302:    **FIREFIGHTER cycles (user, 2026-09-18: \"기존 구조로 처리가 잘 안되는 부분은 Fable, low로 소방수 파견\" · \"단순히\r\n303:    판단만으로는 부족\" · \"트리거링 걸리면 발동하도록\" · \"Low로 첫번, 그 다음 middle로. 그래도 안되면 나한테 판단\r\n304:    요청\").** The runner itself decides, from bgrun logs, never a session or a person: when the SAME recipe\r\n305:    (`tools/recipes/<name>.py`, in command position) ends `BGRUN END rc≠0` / `TIMEOUT` in TWO CONSECUTIVE cycles,\r\n306:    — **or the SAME MISTAKE under another name** (user, 2026-09-18: \"동일 실수 반복하는 것도 판단 조건에 들어가야\"):\r\n307:    the same first failing GATE line (uids stripped) in two consecutive cycles even if the recipe was renamed\r\n308:    v1 → v2, or a retrospective archived in the cycle carrying `VIOLATION: repeated-failure-class` —\r\n309:    the NEXT cycle runs as a whole cycle on **fable / low** (so it can execute, not only advise; it may patch and\r\n310:    run the recipe itself with `MATERIAL=1`). If the same recipe fails again → the next cycle is **fable / medium**;\r\n311:    if it fails a third time → `RUNNER STOP`, the user's judgement is requested; there is never a third\r\n312:    firefighter. Every other rule (1, 1a, 1b, no new process device, peer review, retrospective) holds inside a\r\n313:    firefighter cycle. The user may also order one directly (`--firefighter <recipe>`, first cycle only, as on\r\n314:    2026-09-18 12:59). Self-test: `tools/bench/selftest_cycle_runner_ff.py` (2/2).\r\n315:    **A DEADLINE ENDS THE RUNNER BETWEEN CYCLES, NEVER MID-CYCLE (user, 2026-09-21: \"그냥 셧다운 하지 말고 진행 작업들 마무리하고 종료하는 방향으로. 그래야 다음 싸이클에 정상적으로 작동하지\").** `cycle_runner.py --budget-min` (default 480) is checked only between cycles; the cycle in progress always finishes (retrospective landed, NEXT written, git committed). The `bgrun --max-min` around the runner is the LAST RESORT and is set well above budget + the longest cycle (≈3 h), e.g. `--max-min 720`. A hard kill mid-cycle left a failing log with no review and deadlocked the next launch on 2026-09-21.\r\n316:    **`## NEXT` IS WRITTEN BEFORE THE RETROSPECTIVE IS LAUNCHED — MECHANICAL (user, 2026-09-21: \"NEXT 작성하도록 훅에 강제할 필요성 있을듯\").** The runner snapshots the NEXT section's md5 before spawning a session (`tools/bench/next_snapshot.md5`); `guard_bash.py next_gate()` refuses `retrospective.py` while NEXT still hashes the same. Sessions 58/64/65/66 exited waiting on their retrospective with NEXT unwritten, and the chat rewrote it four times.\r\n317: 3. **Split sessions by JUDGEMENT vs MATERIAL — ADOPTED 2026-09-15** (user, after comparing token costs directly:\r\n318:    *\"정말 필수적으로 고차원적인 판단이 필요한 경우에만 Fable 사용하고 Fable이 판단할 재료들은 opus 혹은 하위 모델로\r\n319:    세션을 잡는게 거의 필수처럼 보이는데?\"* — Opus at high effort is far cheaper than Fable at low). Note this INVERTS\r\n320:    the direction sketched here earlier: the scarce model is spent ONLY on judgement, and Opus is the\r\n321:    **material-preparation** tier, not a lower one — that is where most wall-clock time goes.\r\n322:    - **MATERIAL** (its own session, Opus high by default): measuring API facts (property short-name censuses, class\r\n323:      hierarchy, a VI's terminal names), writing/running recipes that follow an already-verified pattern, log greps,\r\n324:      INDEX/NAMES/STATUS bookkeeping, dispatching peer reviews and collecting their answers, and extracting the bare\r\n325:      facts from a failed run (which gate, which values).\r\n326:    - **JUDGEMENT** (the scarce model; Opus max while the Fable budget is out): design decisions, what to accept from a\r\n327:      review, rule-1a calls, diagnosis when hypotheses genuinely diverge, and designing the discriminating experiment.\r\n328:    - **Failure budget = 2.** A material session that fails twice STOPS, writes the logs and state down, and hands the\r\n329:      problem to a judgement session. (`OpCaseFrames_v0` looked closed-spec and failed five times — grinding is more\r\n330:      expensive than the judgement it avoids.)\r\n331:    - **Judgement sessions stay SHORT**: STATUS.md, the relevant plan document, and a summary of the failing log —\r\n332:      nothing else. A judgement session at 78 % of its context window defeats the whole point.\r\n333:    - **RE-ISSUED 2026-09-16, after the subscription was upgraded — the upgrade does NOT relax this:** *\"Fable의\r\n334:      사용량을 최대한 줄이고, 필요하다면 하부 세션을 늘려서라도 opus 비중을 높이는 게 좋음.\"* Minimise Fable\r\n335:      unconditionally; **spawn more Opus sub-sessions rather than do material work in a Fable session.** The moment\r\n336:      a judgement session is about to write a recipe, read a long log or patch a wrapper, hand off. (Trigger: on\r\n337:      2026-09-16 one Fable session did an entire cycle — recipes, log reads, a 26-wrapper patch — in one context.)\r\n338:    - **The hand-off is mechanical — two agent definitions (user, 2026-09-16 \"에이전트 정의 먼저\"):**\r\n339:      `.claude/agents/material.md` (Opus high: writes/runs recipes and diagnostics, patches tools, dispatches\r\n340:      peers, keeps STATUS; returns a ≤30-line fact summary; failure budget 2) and `.claude/agents/log-reader.md`\r\n341:      (Opus low, read-only: which gate, which values, which line; ≤25 lines). **A judgement session spawns these\r\n342:      via the Agent tool and never runs `tools/recipes/*.py` or `tools/bench/*.py` itself, never reads a whole\r\n343:      log itself.** The judgement session's turns are three kinds only: read STATUS + plan, decide, delegate.\r\n344:    - **A delegation brief states the MEASUREMENT, never the result-dependent ACTION** (2026-09-16, after the\r\n345:      user asked whether a judgement session fed ≤30-line summaries can still judge). Writing \"if A2 removes 1\r\n346:      do X, else do Y\" into a material brief is not delegation — it moves the decision into the session that\r\n347:      is not supposed to make it, before the evidence exists. The material session measures, returns FACTS and\r\n348:      an `OPEN:` line, and stops; the judgement session decides and delegates again. One extra sub-session is\r\n349:      the intended cost. The retrospective asks, per cycle, whether any decision was taken inside a material\r\n350:      session that belonged to judgement — slug `judgement-in-material`, counted by `violations.py` like the\r\n351:      others. The judgement session may always ask `log-reader` for more; ≤30 lines is a default, not a cap.\r\n352:    - **Reports the user reads are WRITTEN BY CODEX, not by Claude** (user, 2026-09-16: *\"보고는 검수를 받는게\r\n353:      아니라 그냥 chatgpt cli에 의존하는게 좋을 것 같은데? 그냥 클로드와 코덱스는 문체가 달라.\"*). For any\r\n354:      paragraph-length report, summary or decision request, Claude hands `.claude/agents/reporter.md` a **fact\r\n355:      list** (numbers, paths, gate outcomes, the question) and shows that text as-is (`peer.ps1 -Kind prose`,\r\n356:      archived under `archive/prose/`, invisible to every gate). ⚠️ **From 2026-09-18 the writer is `claude\r\n357:      -Role prose` = fable/low with a THIN prompt (`--safe-mode`), not codex** (§5, \"Codex's roles move to Claude\r\n358:      sub-sessions\"): the rule exists so the report is not in this session's sentence shapes, and a cell that never\r\n359:      loads this project's rules or context is not this session. Codex stays at `-Agent codex -Kind prose`.\r\n360:      **The dispatch is run by the judgement session\r\n361:      itself, in the FOREGROUND, not by a sub-agent** (2026-09-17, user: \"어떻게 하면 같은 일을 막을 수 있을까\"):\r\n362:      twice a `reporter` sub-agent backgrounded the call and returned, and its exit killed the child before codex\r\n363:      had written a line. The relay is one file write and one ~60 s wait; `guard_bash.py` allows exactly that\r\n364:      shape (`-Kind prose` under bgrun, foreground timeout ≤ 6 min), and it costs no sub-agent tokens. Claude still writes one-line answers (rule 2b),\r\n365:      short status lines and questions of a sentence or two. A proofreading variant was tried first and\r\n366:      rejected the same day — rewriting Claude's sentences keeps Claude's sentence shapes.\r\n367: \r\n368: ### MODEL LADDERS, decided by the runner from files (user, 2026-09-26)\r\n369: \r\n370: *\"지금 3일 안에 사용량 다 써야하는데, 당분간 Fable 5.1 low 사용빈도를 높여보는 건\"* · *\"판정 세션은 필요에 따라서 medium\r\n371: 상위 혹은 fable로 교체하는 것도 생각해보는게 어떨지\"*. Two ladders, both mechanical (`tools/cycle_runner.py`), nobody\r\n372: \"feels\" a level:\r\n373: - **Material sessions:** default **Fable 5.1 low until 2026-09-28 07:00 KST** (the weekly renewal; a one-time app\r\n374:   reminder `revert-material-model-monday` fires then), escalation rungs Fable medium → `decisions_pending`. After\r\n375:   Monday the default returns to `claude-opus-5-5` medium unless the Fable-vs-Opus comparison (result cards carry\r\n376:   `escalation`) says otherwise. Data so far: fable/low delivered L7-1b after two Opus failures (cycles 71/72); a\r\n377:   fable/low JUDGEMENT cycle (87) produced nothing in 6 min.\r\n378: - **Judgement sessions:** level 0 `claude-opus-5-5` medium → 1 Opus high → 2 Fable low → 3 Fable medium → RUNNER STOP\r\n379:   + decision item. Up one level when the last cycle left `next.json` UNCHANGED or its retrospective named a\r\n380:   judgement fault (`inference-over-measurement`, `wrong-ordering`, `judgement-in-material`); back to 0 after a cycle\r\n381:   that delivers. The recipe firefighter (fable/low on a repeated recipe failure) stays; the two never stack above\r\n382:   Fable medium. The level and its reason are a `JUDGE-LADDER` runner-log line and a field of the cycle card.\r\n383: - **Judgement effort A/B until Monday** (user, 2026-09-26: *\"Opus 5.5도 기본을 medium이 좋을지 high가 좋을지도 판단\r\n384:   필요\"*): no cycle so far ran judgement at high, so there is no basis to choose; `cycle_runner --judge-ab` alternates\r\n385:   level-0 effort by cycle parity (odd medium, even high, `JUDGE-AB` log line, `effort` in the cycle card; a triggered\r\n386:   ladder level overrides it) until 2026-09-28 07:00, and the Monday comparison (minutes, cost, deliveries, judgement\r\n387:   slugs, unchanged-NEXT count) sets the default. External prior: Artificial Analysis index Opus 5.5 medium 51 / high\r\n388:   54 at $1.34 / $1.82 per task.\r\n389: \r\n390: ### INTRA-CYCLE ESCALATION — a card that runs out of budget goes to Fable low, then Fable medium, then to the user (user, 2026-09-25)\r\n391: \r\n392: *\"한 싸이클 내부에서도 특정 프로세스가 과하게 오래 걸리거나 반복적으로 실패할 경우 Fable 5.1 낮음 (그래도 안된다면 Fable\r\n393: 5.1 중간) 높여보는 것이 유효할지\"* — asked while cycle 85 spent 105 min building two missing verbs on Opus. The\r\n394: cycle-level firefighter (fable/low after two failing cycles) already exists; this is the same ladder inside a cycle,\r\n395: per card. Trigger: a `task/1` card returns FAIL with its failure budget spent, or exceeds `budget.minutes`. Rung 1:\r\n396: re-issue the card (`escalation: 1`, `retry_of_card`) to `.claude/agents/material-fable-low.md`; rung 2: `escalation:\r\n397: 2` to `material-fable-medium.md`; then `decisions_pending.json` and stop. Two rungs per card, same flags, rules and\r\n398: peers; the result card carries `escalation`, so Opus-vs-Fable outcomes accumulate as data. Evidence so far: cycles\r\n399: 71/72 (fable/low) delivered L7-1b after two Opus failures. This does not relax the standing rule to minimise Fable —\r\n400: it bounds it to a card that Opus has already failed twice.\r\n401: \r\n402: ### Every cycle ends with a RETROSPECTIVE — the peer loop cannot criticise judgement otherwise (user, 2026-09-15)\r\n403: \r\n404: (User: *\"피어 리뷰를 통해 판단 및 실행 구조에 대한 비평은 할 수 없는 것 같아.\"*) The hypothesis-level reviews all\r\n405: pass and the cycle still goes badly, because a peer only ever sees a framing Claude wrote about a failure Claude\r\n406: chose. Nobody asks whether the question was worth asking, whether twenty failures were one failure, or whether a\r\n407: tool should have come first. So the cycle itself is reviewed, from the machine's record rather than from Claude's\r\n408: summary:\r\n409: \r\n410: 1. `py tools/audit_cycle.py` — mechanical compliance, no judgement: bgrun discipline, every run terminated, every\r\n411:    failing log followed by an archived review, every review annotated, the originals untouched, GUI actions\r\n412:    recorded — plus the cost lines (builds, failures, wall-clock) the retrospective argues about.\r\n413: 2. `py tools/retrospective.py --cycle N` — **v2, ADOPTED 2026-09-16** (v1 frozen at `tools/retrospective_v1.py`,\r\n414:    kept runnable for the comparison only; measurement in `tools/bench/retro_v2_comparison.md`). It dispatches a\r\n415:    FIXED question set (repeated failure · missing tool · inference over measurement · rule compliance · ordering ·\r\n416:    what was not reported · judgement inside a material session) plus a DEVICE EFFECT question built mechanically\r\n417:    from `docs/violation-decisions.md`, with the audit output, the cycle's build logs and CLAUDE.md attached.\r\n418:    Claude does not write the questions and does not choose the evidence. v1 SATURATED — cycles 11/12/13 fired 9/9/6\r\n419:    slugs; v2 fired 2/2/1 with three different top faults.\r\n420: 3. **v2's output contract:** the peer names the **ONE most costly structural fault** (at most two), everything else\r\n421:    is prose FINDINGS with no slug, `VIOLATION: none` is a legitimate and expected answer, and each named fault\r\n422:    carries `loss_min`, `loss_usd` (or `?`) and a clock counterfactual:\r\n423:    `VIOLATION: <slug> | loss_min=<n> | loss_usd=<n or ?> | evidence=<file:line>`. Slugs come from the known list\r\n424:    (`repeated-failure-class`, `tool-not-built`, `inference-over-measurement`, `rule-evaded`, `wrong-ordering`,\r\n425:    `unreported-fact`, `scope-creep`, `premature-build`, `judgement-in-material`, `device-failed` — the last two\r\n426:    added 2026-09-16; `device-failed` has **threshold 1**, because a device that let its own fault through is broken,\r\n427:    not unlucky). `py tools/violations.py` parses both v2's form and v1's bare `VIOLATION: <slug>` and counts them\r\n428:    **across retrospectives, from the files**\r\n429:    — because \"add a device when it repeats\" is worthless if Claude is the one counting (the user's question:\r\n430:    *\"회고가 반복해서 위반을 지적하는지는 어떻게 알아?\"*). **At 3 of the same slug the next cycle must build the\r\n431:    mechanical device for it first.** Only the user may lower that threshold. ✅ **THE 2026-09-18 NO-NEW-DEVICE ORDER IS LIFTED (user, 2026-09-24 03:1x: \"새로운 도구 만드는 걸 내가 막아뒀는데, 보니까 계속 구멍이 생기는 것 같아. 루프 판단에 따라 필요한 도구는 만드는 걸 허용할게\") — after cycle 68 lost ~25 min to two missing scripting verbs (wiring to a loop's `i` terminal; no creator for `Not Equal?`/`Select`) and routed around them. A cycle's JUDGEMENT session decides a tool is necessary (it unblocks the stage, or the same class of work will need it again) and a material session builds it in that cycle, under every existing rule (peer-reviewed plan, stagekit, handle-count hygiene, measured before it acts). Deliverable-first ordering still holds: the tool is built because the deliverable needs it. The threshold below resumes with the same words; the suspension text is kept for history.** ⚠️ **(HISTORY) SUSPENDED since 2026-09-18\r\n432:    08:53** by the user's standing order (*\"장치는 더 민들지 말고 계속 진행\"*, **user, 2026-09-18 08:53** — cited by\r\n433:    DATE, never by a STATUS line number, which every relocation moves): while it\r\n434:    stands, a slug reaching 3 is recorded as a FINDING in `docs/violation-decisions.md` and the next cycle builds\r\n435:    NO device; the threshold resumes the moment the user lifts the order. **EXCEPTION (user, 2026-09-22: \"Jev 건은 예외로 추가하도록 하고\"): devices that put TypeSafe's Jev (a typed-decision model, `docs/jev-integration-plan.md`) behind an EXISTING decision point — the firefighter trigger, the failed-prediction review gate, log classification, plan checks — are allowed under the no-new-device order, each one MEASURED on a labelled set before it is switched on and introduced as an ADVISORY signal first.** Jev scripts (`tools/jev*.py`, `tools/bench/jev_*.py`) are EXEMPT from the failed-prediction and material gates (user, 2026-09-22 \"Jev는 면제\") — they touch no LabVIEW. Thresholds in force: review discharge p≥0.80 (active), firefighter veto p≤0.30 (active), triage/NEXT/prior-art advisory only. **SECOND WAVE APPROVED (user, 2026-09-22 17:3x: \"훌륭하네. 이거 다 적용해보자\") — `docs/jev-integration-plan.md` '2차 후보' 1~7, all of them, including the RULE CHANGE in #1: a failed prediction goes first to the JEV REVIEW LADDER (3-way: our-script-bug / already-reviewed-class / new-problem); only `new-problem` (or no key / unknown band) owes the Opus `hypothesis` review; the other two pass with a logged `JEV-LADDER` line and a citation. Each insertion is measured on a labelled set before it acts; #4 (per-command drift) and #7 (Pre-decided contradictions) are advisory; #6 (5-sample consensus) is the default for every gate call.** **ONE REVIEW PER ROW PER CYCLE (user, 2026-09-22 18:xx \"전부 적용해보자\", after the measured cost: reviews were 30 % of cycle time, 8 in one cycle, mostly a chain of failures on ONE stage row): a failed prediction on the SAME script (basename with `_vN` stripped) that already has an ANSWERED adversary review archived within the last 6 h is DISCHARGED by that review — `guard_peer` cites it (`RULE-SAME-ROW`), no second review is bought. A different script, or a review older than 6 h, still owes its own review. Also: the Jev drift advisory (#4) judges against the WHOLE `## NEXT`, not only its first act; the 10 Pre-decided contradiction suspects from #7 are checked and annotated, not left as a list.** **Second exception (user, 2026-09-22 \"좋아. 다음 사이클에 추가하도록\"): the stage-script library `tools/stagekit.py` (Pre-decided 93) — the repeated skeleton of stage/diagnostic scripts as one verified module, so a stage file is inputs only.** **BUILT 2026-09-22 16:0x (725 lines, self-test 32/0, a 595-line probe re-cut in 120 lines matched 13/13). From now on every new stage or diagnostic is a ≤120-line file on `tools/stagekit.py`; a free-standing multi-hundred-line script is the pattern the user rejected.** Resolved 2026-09-18 by the cycle-26\r\n436:    judgement session after `doc_ingest` reported this line contradicting STATUS — the later user statement wins.\r\n437: 4. `tools/hooks/guard_cycle.py` refuses the next RECIPE build while the previous cycle's logs have no newer\r\n438:    retrospective, or while a slug is at threshold. Diagnostics, docs, peers and the retrospective itself pass.\r\n439: \r\n440: ### When a diagnosis is GUESSED twice, build the reader (2026-09-15)\r\n441: \r\n442: The sibling of \"when a rule is broken twice, move it into a hook\". On 2026-09-15 three consecutive `ExecState 0`\r\n443: failures were diagnosed by inference — branch wire, downcast, transient Remove Bad Wires — each costing a peer review\r\n444: and a rebuild, because the fleet has no way to ask LabVIEW *why* a VI is broken. Each was 3 minutes of measurement\r\n445: dressed as 20 minutes of reasoning. **Rule: the second time a class of failure is explained by inference rather than\r\n446: read from the machine, the next build is the READER for it, not another attempt at the thing that failed.** Readers\r\n447: identified this way: **`Wire.Is Broken?` 6371004 IS BUILT** and measured (`docs/NAMES.md:902-911`, 2026-09-17) — but ONLY inside the connect ops, there is no stand-alone read-only `Wire.Is Broken?` op (STATUS 2026-09-25, `docs/NAMES.md:1081-1089`); use the connect ops' read-back instead of inferring why a wire is bad; `VI.Get Errors` (method 452) is still unbuilt and is probably unreachable over our COM path (absent from the exported `VirtualInstrument` ActiveX interface — `archive/peer/2026-09-18-fstunnel-v1-b4-execstate0-codex.md`), so it is off the critical path. Corrected 2026-09-18 by the cycle-22 judgement session after a `-Dual` review found this line stale.\r\n448: \r\n449: ### Stages are SIMULATED and PRE-RUN OFFLINE before LabVIEW touches them (user, 2026-09-24)\r\n450: \r\n451: *\"미리 사전계획 해서 옮길 vi들 미리 정리하고 만들 struct 미리 계산한 다음 그 결과 어떻게 될지 미리 예측 … Sequential하게\r\n452: 각 단계들을 시뮬레이션 하여 temporary 파일로 저장한 후에 각 사이클의 finalized 플랜을 두는게 맞는듯.\"* Said after the\r\n453: loop-1.7 split failed 6 of 9 LabVIEW runs (≈49 min of LabVIEW, several times that in diagnosis) on problems that were\r\n454: all knowable offline. Eight decisions; design and build order in `docs/stage-simulator-plan.md`:\r\n455: \r\n456: 1. every stage script is DRY-RUN first (COM stubbed); 2. an OFFLINE PRE-RUN (every row decided, every terminal\r\n457: addressable from the graph — unwired ones by owner node → terminal list → uid echo, rows == plan rows);\r\n458: 3. error-cluster and accumulator chains are copied from S1 (`RULE-CHAIN-S1`), never asked of Jev; 4. a failed run\r\n459: invalidates the pre-run records; 5. every cycle starts by reading the bed's LabVIEW Error List through the GUI,\r\n460: each item double-clicked to its location (*\"GUI로 에러 내용 확인하고 각 에러 더블클릭하면 에러 위치로 이동해서\r\n461: 보여주거든\"*); 6. a move's cut set and reconnect table are computed from the graph before the move and checked after;\r\n462: 7. the whole stage is simulated action by action (temporary graph per step, symbolic ids bound by a before/after\r\n463: terminal-table diff) and finalized at `computation_diff` 0, then executed once with a per-step comparison;\r\n464: 8. row content comes ONLY from the finalized plan file — a recipe never re-types a uid or terminal name.\r\n465: **Python computes, Jev picks per-row mechanisms, the LLM designs and takes low-confidence rows.** 1/2/4/7 are\r\n466: enforced by the launch gate in `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py`, 5 by `cycle_runner.py`'s `errorlist_hook`, 3/6/7/8 by\r\n467: stagekit gates; this section records only the why.\r\n468: \r\n469: ### Big or blocked work is SPLIT into steps that each SAVE an intermediate artefact (user, 2026-09-19)\r\n470: \r\n471: *\"앞으로도 프로젝트 방향성에 관해서 큰 덩어리의 프로젝트는, 혹은 병목이 생긴 부분에서는, 세부적으로 쪼개서 중간과정\r\n472: 저장하며 진행하는 방식으로 규칙 수정 필요할듯.\"* — said after ten overnight route-B attempts (v3→v7, 5 cycles, ≈$166)\r\n473: edited a VI copy entirely in LabVIEW memory, died at the same re-wiring stage every time, and left **no file to look\r\n474: at** (the \"preserved\" crash copies were byte-identical to the untouched original). Applies to everything still ahead\r\n475: (EMCCD connection/sync, display, …), not only D1.\r\n476: \r\n477: 1. **A step is not done until it has left a file.** Every build stage saves its intermediate VI/data under `claudeDev`,\r\n478:    records its md5, and the next stage starts FROM THAT FILE — in a fresh LabVIEW instance when the stage uses VI\r\n479:    Scripting. The user must always have something to open.\r\n480: 2. **Default granularity = one saved artefact per natural stage** (copy → structures → moves → re-wiring in batches of\r\n481:    10–15 rows → census → final save). Not one script per wire (*\"일일히 배선 하나하나 별도 스크립트를 쓰는 건 낭비\"*).\r\n482: 3. **Re-splitting is triggered, not felt** (user: the decision is the judgement session's, the trigger is the\r\n483:    machine's): the same stage failing twice at the same place, or a stage that ends without a saved artefact ⇒ the\r\n484:    next cycle's FIRST act is a **decomposition plan** for that stage (one page: sub-steps, each's saved file name and\r\n485:    pass criterion), prior-art-reviewed once, then executed step by step by material sessions. A full-length retry under\r\n486:    a new file name (`_v8`) is forbidden. `cycle_runner.py` counts renamed recipes as the same recipe.\r\n487: 4. A new large stage (e.g. EMCCD sync) starts with its step list and saved-file list written into the plan; no build\r\n488:    before that list exists.\r\n489: 6. **A BROKEN INTERMEDIATE MAY BE SAVED BY GUI Ctrl+S (user, 2026-09-22: \"저장 허용함.\").** A `claudeDev` stage artefact that is broken BY DESIGN (its missing rows belong to the next stage, `ExecState` 0) is saved through `gui_save` (block-diagram window fronted and click-probed, Ctrl+S, mtime verified) with `-Exception Approved -Evidence \"user 2026-09-22 broken-intermediate save\"`, because COM `SaveInstrument` hangs on a broken VI — the scripted route is verified unreachable for this class. Such a file is NEVER run and never cold-loaded headless. Not for originals (rule 1) and not for the final deliverable, which must reach `ExecState` 1 and save by script.\r\n490: 5. **Reference hygiene is a precondition, not an afterthought**: an op that traverses without `Close Reference` is\r\n491:    repaired before it is used in a staged build (20 consecutive calls in one script, handle count flat ±100).\r\n492: \r\n493: ### The work cycle (user, 2026-08-31)\r\n494: \r\n495: 1) **Plan, and peer-review the plan**; 2) write the WHOLE build as **one long script file**\r\n496: (Python/bash — e.g. `tools/recipes/*.py`), every name resolved from `docs/NAMES.md` at planning\r\n497: time; 3) execute the file as a batch; 4) read the results report, revise; 5) repeat. No mid-run\r\n498: name discovery; no per-step verification round-trips. Target: replay speed, discovery paid up\r\n499: front.\r\n500: \r\n501: ### Tooling\r\n502: \r\n503: **Hooks (mechanical rules, 2026-09-04)** in `.claude/settings.json`: `tools/hooks/answer_first.py`\r\n504: (UserPromptSubmit) re-injects rule 2b on every user message; `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py`\r\n505: (PreToolUse Bash|PowerShell) blocks any LabVIEW-touching command run in the foreground without a\r\n506: timeout ≤ 30 s — background it instead. Prose rules fade after compaction; code rules do not\r\n507: (the GUI gate in lv_gui.ps1 ended six rounds of GUI-creep). When a rule is broken twice, move it\r\n508: into a hook. Hook commands use forward-slash paths (backslashes are escape-interpreted). **Project hooks also run\r\n509: inside every `claude -p` cell spawned from this directory** — a hook that gives advice (\"stalled client,\r\n510: stop the task\") will be acted on by that cell; gate such hooks on an env var the spawner sets\r\n511: (`BENCH_CELL`), as lv_stallcheck.ps1 now does.\r\n512: \r\n513: **Patch files with the Edit/Write tools, never with a `py - <<'EOF'` heredoc** (2026-09-09: three heredoc patches\r\n514: in one session died on `\\U`/`\\N` escapes or literal NUL bytes, and because the launch line followed on a new line\r\n515: the chain ran UNPATCHED each time — 20 min lost per round). A heredoc is fine for read-only one-liners only.\r\n516: \r\n517: All screenshot/window/mouse/keyboard/checksum operations go through\r\n518: [tools/lv_gui.ps1](tools/lv_gui.ps1) — never inline `Add-Type`. `.claude/settings.json`\r\n519: pre-approves it **only** in the exact form `& .\\tools\\lv_gui.ps1 -Action ...` from the project root\r\n520: — a `cd`, a variable, or an absolute path breaks the allowlist and stalls unattended work. The same\r\n521: settings **deny** Edit/Write on `*.vi`, `*.ctl`, `*.lvlib`, `*.lvproj` (compiled binaries; every\r\n522: legitimate change happens inside LabVIEW) — defense-in-depth for rule 1.\r\n523: \r\n524: ## 4. Documentation is layered: short top index, topic files below, archive unread\r\n525: \r\n526: (User, 2026-08-27 + 2026-08-31 \"계층화\".) **Active docs** state what is true now, in as little text\r\n527: as a cold start needs: `STATUS.md` stays under one screen and only points; verified facts go to\r\n528: topic files (`docs/`, skill `references/`); narrative goes to [archive/](archive/) — nothing is\r\n529: deleted, only moved. **Do not read `archive/` in normal work**; open a specific file only when the\r\n530: active docs are ambiguous, and say why. When any working doc needs scrolling to find the current\r\n531: state, push content down a layer immediately, not at the next cleanup. Scratch artefacts\r\n532: (throwaway VIs, build targets) are created and deleted in the same operation.\r\n533: \r\n534: **Concrete threshold (2026-09-15, after STATUS.md reached 651 lines):** STATUS.md over ~100 lines means the cycle\r\n535: narrative has crept back in — move it to `archive/<date>-status-<topic>.md` and leave lock, hardware permission,\r\n536: current state, OPEN items and NEXT. The narrative is never rewritten, only relocated.\r\n537: \r\n538: ### Documents are LINTED by code and INGESTED by a model every cycle (user, 2026-09-16)\r\n539: \r\n540: *\"특정 주기마다 .md 파일들 ingest 및 lint 하는 규약 필요해보임\"* — after STATUS reached 526 lines, CLAUDE.md carried a\r\n541: stale slug list and two docs described one wire two ways. Cadence is **per cycle / per run, never weekly**\r\n542: (*\"주 단위보다는 싸이클 단위 혹은 실제 실행 단위가 적절해보임\"*). \"Ingest\" means **a model reads and refreshes\r\n543: state** — NOT an index; the index was measured and rejected (`tools/bench/priorart_scores.md`).\r\n544: \r\n545: | layer | what | model | when |\r\n546: |---|---|---|---|\r\n547: | **lint** | `tools/doc_lint.py` — frontmatter valid; every cited path / `file:line` exists; STATUS ≤ ~100 lines; one `status: current` plan; `supersedes:` targets not still current; review dispositions not placeholders; unmarked decision sentences (warn) | none (a `.py`) | every cycle close, run by `audit_cycle` |\r\n548: | **ingest — changed docs** | `tools/doc_ingest.py --cycle N` — the claude peer as rule/consistency auditor (`peer.ps1 -Role ingest`, archived to `archive/ingest/`, invisible to every gate) over the cycle's changed files (audit C7 list): contradictions between them and with the active docs | **Sonnet** | every cycle close |\r\n549: | **ingest — all active docs** | `tools/doc_ingest.py --full --model opus` — same audit over all of `docs/` + STATUS + CLAUDE.md | **Opus** | every 5 cycles (same rhythm as the outcome review) |\r\n550: | **resolving** which document is right | — | judgement session | when the ingest reports a contradiction |\r\n551: \r\n552: ## 5. Peer agents (Codex, Gemini) — Claude manages, they only advise\r\n553: \r\n554: Read-only research sub-agents; their brief is [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). **Dispatch only through\r\n555: [`tools/peer.ps1`](tools/peer.ps1)** (`-Agent codex|gemini|claude [-Kind review|fact] -Slug <name>\r\n556: -Task \"...\"` — nothing else validates). It enforces read-only, bounds every call with a timeout, classifies the outcome, and\r\n557: archives the exchange. Screenshots and reporter text may be attached; **never `.vi` files**. Attach\r\n558: a ~3-line \"already ruled out\" block for diagnostic questions; none for pure API facts. **A peer\r\n559: answer is a hypothesis** — confirm against the machine or the vendor's own files; two models\r\n560: agreeing is not confirmation. Call them *before building something expensive* and whenever a\r\n561: diagnosis is about to drive real work; dispatch needs no approval and has no call limit. On quota\r\n562: exhaustion stop calling that agent for the session — degrade to working without it, never to\r\n563: waiting.\r\n564: \r\n565: ### Review has THREE layers, and only the third asks whether the work was worth doing (2026-09-15)\r\n566: \r\n567: | layer | asks | fires | enforced by |\r\n568: |---|---|---|---|\r\n569: | hypothesis | is this diagnosis right? | every failed prediction | `guard_peer.py` |\r\n570: | cycle | was this cycle run well? | end of every cycle | `guard_cycle.py` + `retrospective.py` |\r\n571: | **outcome** | **did any of it move the deliverable?** | **every 5 cycles or 7 days** | `guard_cycle.py` + `outcome_review.py` |\r\n572: \r\n573: The first two both ask HOW; a cycle can be run impeccably and still be the wrong cycle (user,\r\n574: 2026-09-15: \"주기적으로 프로젝트의 성과를 판단하는 피어도 필요하지 않을까\"). The outcome layer asks\r\n575: against `project-requirements/`: what can the user RUN today that they could not before; which\r\n576: numbered requirement has not moved since the project began; is the cost-to-product ratio defensible;\r\n577: if the user had to run an experiment next week, would they use the original VI or anything we built.\r\n578: Its reviewer was **codex, never claude** — \"was this worth doing\" is the question a reviewer sharing our priors is\r\n579: worst at. ⚠️ **CHANGED 2026-09-18, a TRIAL (see \"Codex's roles move to Claude sub-sessions\" below): the reviewer is\r\n580: `peer.ps1 -Agent claude -Role outcome` = FABLE / medium, run `--safe-mode` so the cell never loads CLAUDE.md,\r\n581: STATUS.md's framing or the plan documents into its system prompt.** The original objection is answered by the THIN\r\n582: PROMPT rather than by the vendor — what made codex right here was not sharing our priors, and a cell that reads\r\n583: only `project-requirements/` and the counted output does not share them either. `-Agent codex -Kind fact` is one\r\n584: flag away if the trial fails. **Fired three times (2026-09-15, 2026-09-16, 2026-09-18: zero runnable experimental VIs every time); the\r\n585: user re-planned to DELIVERY-FIRST on 2026-09-16 (\"A로 진행하자\") — `docs/cycle15-plan.md` — with the boundary\r\n586: *\"여기는 중간 과정일 뿐 결국에는 최종 스텝으로 나가야함\"*: delivery is ordering, the seven-loop restructure is\r\n587: still the goal.** **An `OUTCOME-VIOLATION` is NOT answered by building a device** (that is the\r\n588: process rule, and answering goal drift with another tool is how the drift happened): the next cycle\r\n589: becomes a **delivery** cycle, and on repetition the work stops for a re-plan with the user. **AMENDED by the user 2026-09-23 (\"83번으로 가자\", resolving Pre-decided 44 vs 83): once the user has ANSWERED a repeated outcome verdict (as on 2026-09-20, \"계속\"), a further repetition of the SAME verdict does NOT stop the runner — the session marks the escalation in STATUS and the interactive chat reports it; the work stops only when the verdict's CONTENT changes (a new violation line, or a requirement moving backwards).** Keep the\r\n590: layer to one script, one dispatch and one gate line — the user's explicit budget for it. **STEERING CARD (user, 2026-09-24: \"아웃컴 리뷰에 조향카드 부여하는 것 동의\"):** a repeated outcome verdict now also emits a `steer/1` card (the next cycle's required act, tied to `docs/goalmap.json` ids); the judgement session FOLLOWS it or REFUSES it with cited evidence in `next.json`; two refusals of the same item stop the runner and put it on `tools/bench/decisions_pending.json`. **RETRY CAP (user, same day: \"재시도 상한도 동의함. 다만 숫자 … 데이터를 쌓아가면서 조정\"):** one stage's LabVIEW runs per cycle are capped (`tools/stage_prerun.py` `RETRY_CAP`, start 2); a further run needs a judgement card; per-stage run counts are recorded so the number is tuned from data.\r\n591: \r\n592: **The fourth layer — PRIOR ART (\"has this already been done here?\", `prior_art_review.py`, gated by the same\r\n593: `guard_cycle.py`) — stops the work on any verdict other than `novel`, and there are exactly TWO releases, each\r\n594: written into the review file itself and each paid for with a citation:**\r\n595: \r\n596: | release | means | conditions (all machine-checked) |\r\n597: |---|---|---|\r\n598: | `REFUTED: <slug> - <file>:<line> says X, which does not cover Y because …` | the review is **wrong** | open the cited file and show in writing that it does not cover this case. Not \"I think otherwise\" |\r\n599: | `FIXED: <slug> - <path>:<line> - <one sentence saying what changed>` | the review is **right and the work already changed** | (a) `<path>` exists under the project, (b) it was last changed **after** the review (its frontmatter date; the file's own stamp if it carries none), (c) the line sits under `## What was done with it` in the review file |\r\n600: \r\n601: `FIXED:` was added 2026-09-16 (tested: valid releases; nonexistent path does not; older-than-the-review path does\r\n602: not). Until then the gate's own refusal said \"if the review is right, change the plan instead\" while accepting only\r\n603: the branch where the review is WRONG — so a round whose findings were all correct and all acted on could not\r\n604: release the build it had just improved. An assertion is not a refutation and **a promise is not a fix**; the\r\n605: citation is the currency, and `CYCLE_GUARD_OFF` is never the answer.\r\n606: \r\n607: **The peer must be asked to REFUTE — now mechanical.** `peer.ps1 -Kind review` (the default) REFUSES\r\n608: a task carrying confirm-bait (\"please confirm\", \"sanity check\", \"do you agree\", \"확인 부탁\") and\r\n609: APPENDS the adversarial instruction set (strongest reason it is wrong · an alternative explanation ·\r\n610: what would falsify it · the cheapest discriminating test). `-Kind fact` exempts a pure API-fact\r\n611: question. Cycle 7's retrospective found prompts saying \"BRIEF CONFIRM\"; a rule about how a question\r\n612: is *phrased* belongs in the dispatcher, not in good intentions.\r\n613: \r\n614: **The claude peer cannot discharge a failed prediction.** It is the rule/consistency auditor, so\r\n615: `guard_peer.py` requires the lifting exchange to come from codex or gemini **and** to have ended\r\n616: `ANSWERED` — a TIMEOUT/QUOTA/ERROR exchange is exactly what this file calls \"told you nothing\", and\r\n617: the gate used to accept one.\r\n618: \r\n619: ### External search is MANDATORY — the ladder only decides who runs it\r\n620: \r\n621: (User, three times, finally: \"멀티 에이전트 활용을 하던 아니면 너 혼자 답변을 하던 외부 검색 세션을\r\n622: 필수로 넣도록\".) Every factual question about a tool, API, error or capability gets an external\r\n623: search — by a peer or by Claude, never skipped. The error being stamped out is **false confidence\r\n624: from local evidence**: absence in what you happen to be looking at is not evidence of absence.\r\n625: **Trigger sentence:** the moment you are about to write \"X is not possible\", \"our tools cannot\r\n626: reach that\", or \"so we fall back to GUI clicking\" — stop and search. Claims about *our own tools*\r\n627: are factual claims, the ones you are most likely to be wrong about.\r\n628: \r\n629: ### A FAILED PREDICTION triggers mandatory peer review — all three parts\r\n630: \r\n631: (User, 2026-08-30: \"가설을 세워서 결과와 맞지 않으면 피어리뷰 과정을 필수로 넣자. 내가 말한 피어리뷰는\r\n632: 멀티에이전트 검색 및 감시, 더해서 advocate 과정이야\".) **A recurring error alert is a failed prediction too**\r\n633: (user, 2026-09-14, on the repeated \"STALLED LabVIEW client\" hook messages: \"이런 에러들도 반복되는 것 같으니\r\n634: 피어리뷰 반드시 필요하겠어. 규율에 적용하도록\") — mechanically: `tools/lv_stallcheck.ps1` writes\r\n635: `tools/bench/stall_pid<N>.log` (`STALL:` line) and `tools/hooks/guard_peer.py` blocks the next build until\r\n636: a peer review newer than that record is archived. When any other error class shows up a second time, give\r\n637: it the same treatment in the same session: a record the gate can see, not a sentence here. **The gate reads build\r\n638: logs only — `tools/bench/peer_*.log` transcripts are excluded** (2026-09-15: a reviewer's own sentence, \"Fail the\r\n639: build on inequality…\", blocked the very build that review had approved; review logs are evidence, never the thing\r\n640: under test). The moment you predicted X and observed\r\n641: not-X, the explanation you form is a fresh hypothesis built under pressure. Peer review then means\r\n642: **all three together**: (1) multi-agent search asked to *attack* the claim; (2) **monitoring** —\r\n643: watch the dispatch and classify its outcome; a call that failed, timed out or hit quota told you\r\n644: *nothing* (an `-Agent agy` ValidateSet typo once died instantly and would have passed for a\r\n645: completed review); (3) the devil's-advocate pass below. **Your own successful discriminating test\r\n646: does not discharge this** — it confirms the experiment you thought to run; the peer's job is to\r\n647: attack the framing.\r\n648: \r\n649: ### The devil's-advocate pass — search catches ignorance; this catches false knowledge\r\n650: \r\n651: (User, 2026-08-29: \"외부 검색만이 아니라 자체적으로 devil's advocate이 필요하겠는데\".) Before a\r\n652: diagnosis or plan drives real work — and at every \"X is impossible\", before expensive construction,\r\n653: before destructive steps — argue against it. Two requirements keep it from being theatre: **it must\r\n654: end in a discriminating test** (name an alternative cause, name what would falsify the hypothesis,\r\n655: run the cheapest separator — comparing options is not a test); and **a peer used as adversary must\r\n656: be asked to refute, not confirm**. Health check: if the user is the one raising the objections, the\r\n657: internal pass is not running.\r\n658: \r\n659: ### Name the level of verification — structural is not functional\r\n660: \r\n661: (User, 2026-08-29: \"데이터 넣어보지 않았는데 어떻게 확인한 거야?\") **Structural** = the artefact is\r\n662: well-formed and legal (object counts, owners, `ExecState == 1` — the compiler's verdict, nothing\r\n663: more). **Functional** = the right numbers when real data flows through. A build that has never been\r\n664: run is not verified, however many counts agree. State the level explicitly in STATUS and in every\r\n665: summary.\r\n666: \r\n667: ### The research ladder — one rung at a time, and it always has a bottom\r\n668: \r\n669: | | reads files | reads the web | model |\r\n670: |---|---|---|---|\r\n671: | **codex** | ✅ project dir, read-only sandbox | ✅ | `gpt-5.6-sol` / medium, pinned in peer.ps1 (user, 2026-09-15). **Still selectable, no longer any default — weekly quota 9 % on 2026-09-18** |\r\n672: | **agy (gemini)** | ❌ | ✅ | RETIRED from every default/fallback 2026-09-22 (user: roles delegated to claude; headless permission auto-deny). Explicit `-Agent gemini` only |\r\n673: | **claude** | ✅ project dir (the thin roles: only what they choose to read), plan mode + acting tools denied | per role | **`-Role` decides**: `audit` sonnet · `ingest` sonnet · `priorart` claude-opus-5-5/medium · `hypothesis` claude-opus-5-5/high +web (user table 2026-09-23; was opus/high · opus/max) · `fact` **fable/low +web, thin** · `outcome` **fable/medium +web, thin** · `prose` **fable/low, thin** |\r\n674: \r\n675: The claude peer was added 2026-09-15 on the user's direction (\"claude 하위 세션도 peer review에 참여\r\n676: 시키는게 좋겠어\") so the review structure survives an external quota outage. It is the one peer that\r\n677: spends the SAME subscription as the main session. **THIN** = dispatched with `--safe-mode`, so the cell skips\r\n678: CLAUDE.md, skills, plugins, hooks and MCP; that fixed context load, not the model, was the ~$1.5–3 a peer call\r\n679: cost. Web is now per role, not banned outright — `hypothesis`, `fact` and `outcome` have `WebSearch`/`WebFetch`;\r\n680: `audit`, `ingest`, `priorart` and `prose` still have none.\r\n681: \r\n682: One peer per question (the others only to cross-check an answer about to drive expensive construction).\r\n683: Fallback order from 2026-09-22 (user: \"Gemini 역할도 claude에게 위임\" — agy auto-denies its `command` permission headless, so it is dead in unattended runs): **claude → Claude's own research session**; gemini stays selectable by an explicit `-Agent gemini` only, with codex reachable by an\r\n684: explicit `-Agent codex` while its quota lasts. If every peer is exhausted, Claude still runs the external research\r\n685: itself — \"no peer available\" never means going straight to experimenting. Waiting for a quota to renew is never\r\n686: the right move.\r\n687: \r\n688: ### Codex's roles move to Claude sub-sessions — TRIAL, user's decision 2026-09-18\r\n689: \r\n690: > *\"Codex 잔여량이 생각보다 얼마 남지 않음. 주간 한도 9% 남았음. 아무래도 Codex가 수행중인 역할을 fable로\r\n691: > 구동하는게 어떨까 싶음.\"* … *\"우선은 지금 말한 방법으로 몇 번 돌려보자\"*\r\n692: \r\n693: A trial, not a settled rule: run it a few times, then decide. What changed, all inside `peer.ps1`'s role table:\r\n694: \r\n695: | dispatch | before | from 2026-09-18 |\r\n696: |---|---|---|\r\n697: | `-Kind fact`, no `-Agent` | codex by hand | **claude `-Role fact`** — fable/low, thin, web |\r\n698: | `-Kind prose`, no `-Agent` | codex (`reporter`) | **claude `-Role prose`** — fable/low, thin |\r\n699: | `outcome_review.py` | `-Agent codex` | **claude `-Role outcome`** — fable/medium, thin, web |\r\n700: | failed prediction | `-Dual` (codex **and** opus/max) | **`-Agent claude -Role hypothesis` SINGLE arm**; `-Dual` stays available |\r\n701: | `prior_art_review.py`, `doc_ingest.py`, `retrospective.py` | unchanged | unchanged |\r\n702: \r\n703: **D3 is amended**: `guard_peer.py` now accepts an ANSWERED `claude` exchange whose `role:` is `hypothesis`\r\n704: (opus / effort max) as discharging a failed prediction, alongside codex and gemini. Every other claude role still\r\n705: cannot — it is the rule/consistency audit, not a framing adversary. Check a routing change without spending a\r\n706: call: `tools/peer.ps1 -Kind fact -Slug x -Task \"...\" -DryRun` prints agent, role, model, effort, tools and the\r\n707: archive path and dispatches nothing.\r\n708: \r\n709: ### Hypothesis reviews are being moved to an Opus/max Claude peer — measured first (user, 2026-09-17)\r\n710: \r\n711: GPT usage was higher than the user expected (*\"GPT 사용량이 생각보다 적지 않은데\"*). Decision: try the failed-\r\n712: prediction (hypothesis) review on a Claude sub-session under **codex's exact constraints** (read-only, adversarial\r\n713: preamble, timeout, archive, gates) with **Opus at effort MAX** (*\"opus는 high 보다 더 높게 잡아도 문제 없을 것\r\n714: 같은데\"*) and **web search enabled** for that role. Not Fable (cost rule). codex stays for the OUTCOME review and\r\n715: as the second opinion when a Claude review passes a claim about our own tools. **The next five failed predictions\r\n716: go to BOTH; D3 (\"a claude peer cannot discharge a failed prediction\") is amended only after that comparison.**\r\n717: Implementation: `peer.ps1` role `hypothesis` (opus/max, WebSearch allowed) + a dual-dispatch switch; the archive's\r\n718: cost lines are the comparison data.\r\n719: \r\n720: ✅ **BUILT AND SELF-TESTED 2026-09-17** (`tools/bench/peer_dual_selftest.log`, `BGRUN END rc=0 after 100s`):\r\n721: `tools/peer.ps1 -Agent claude -Role hypothesis` = **opus / effort max** with `--allowedTools WebSearch WebFetch\r\n722: Read Glob Grep` (the only claude role with the web; every other one keeps rule 5's \"claude peer has no web\"), and\r\n723: **`-Dual`** re-invokes the script twice on one `-TaskFile`, archiving `<date>-<slug>-codex.md` and\r\n724: `<date>-<slug>-opus.md`. Both arms ANSWERED on the self-test; the cost lines are\r\n725: `codex 26 s` (no cost line — the CLI reports none) vs **`opus $1.8621, in 10 / out 4,581 / cache-create 158,866 /\r\n726: cache-read 258,782, 70 s, 11 turns`**. ⚠️ `-Dual` exits with **codex's** return code.\r\n727: ✅ **SUPERSEDED 2026-09-18 by the trial above**: the comparison stopped early because codex's weekly quota reached\r\n728: 9 %. **D3 IS AMENDED** — `guard_peer.py` accepts `-Agent claude -Role hypothesis` (ANSWERED, opus/max) on its own,\r\n729: so a failed-prediction review is now a **SINGLE claude arm**, not `-Dual`. `-Dual` remains available and is the\r\n730: right call when a claim about our OWN tools needs a second opinion that does not share our priors.\r\n731: \r\n732: ### Archiving peer exchanges — and the one exception to rule 4\r\n733: \r\n734: Every exchange goes to `archive/peer/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` (question, answer, source URLs);\r\n735: conclusions that changed direction get copied into an active doc. **Exception:** check\r\n736: `archive/peer/` for the same question before re-asking — re-asking wastes quota the session may\r\n737: need later.\r\n738: \r\n739: ## Where to actually look\r\n740: \r\n741: - [STATUS.md](STATUS.md) — lock, current state, next actions. Read first, always. One screen.\r\n742: - [docs/NAMES.md](docs/NAMES.md) — verified terminal/label strings (newlines are real). Check\r\n743:   before every wiring call.\r\n744: - `.claude/skills/labview-automation/` — ALL LabVIEW technique (SKILL.md index + 4 references).\r\n745:   Invoke the skill before any LabVIEW work.\r\n746: - [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md) — the rig. [Requests.md](Requests.md) — the user's ground\r\n747:   rules verbatim. [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) — the peers' brief; keep it short and true.\r\n748: - [archive/](archive/) — history; see rule 4.\r\n749: \r\n750: \r\n751: ---\r\n752: \r\n753: ---\r\n754: name: material\r\n755: description: MATERIAL session (CLAUDE.md §3 \"judgement vs material\") — writes and runs recipes/diagnostics, patches tools, dispatches peers, keeps STATUS/INDEX, and returns a SHORT factual summary. Model opus, effort high. This is where every LabVIEW-touching task goes; the calling (judgement) session never runs recipes itself.\r\n756: model: fable\r\n757: effort: low\r\n758: ---\r\n759: \r\n760: <!-- DATED MODEL NOTE (user 2026-09-26, card chat-M1): material runs on **fable / low UNTIL 2026-09-28 07:00 KST**\r\n761: (use the weekly quota before Monday's renewal); from then on it goes BACK to `model: claude-opus-5-5`,\r\n762: `effort: medium`. Escalation while this note stands: fable/low -> material-fable-medium -> decisions_pending\r\n763: (tools/cycle_prompt.md, INTRA-CYCLE ESCALATION). Reverting the two lines above is judgement/chat work. -->\r\n764: \r\n765: You are a **material session** of this project (CLAUDE.md §3, \"Split sessions by JUDGEMENT vs MATERIAL\",\r\n766: re-issued 2026-09-16: *\"Fable의 사용량을 최대한 줄이고, 필요하다면 하부 세션을 늘려서라도 opus 비중을 높이는 게\r\n767: 좋음\"*). The session that spawned you is the scarce judgement model. Your job is to do the material work\r\n768: completely and hand back **facts, not narrative**, so the caller spends as few tokens as possible.\r\n769: \r\n770: ## Session protocol v1 — your prompt is ONE line, your answer is ONE JSON object\r\n771: \r\n772: (`docs/session-protocol.md`, user-approved 2026-09-24.) Your prompt is `CARD tools/bench/cards/task_<id>.json` — a\r\n773: `task/1` card: goal, inputs (with md5), pass criteria, outputs, **flags**, budget, rules. Read the card; it replaces a\r\n774: prose brief. Prose that arrives beside it does not widen it.\r\n775: \r\n776: 1. **Your FIRST command, before any other tool call, is** `py tools/protocol.py bind <card path>` (Bash, timeout\r\n777:    ≤ 30000). The hook records `{your agent_id: card}`; until then every call you make is refused, and after it every\r\n778:    call is checked against the card's `flags` — `labview` none/read/build, `gui`, `hardware` none/gate, `run_vi`,\r\n779:    `write` globs (plus your own `result_<id>.json` and %TEMP%), `status_edit`, `git_commit`, `peers`. A refusal\r\n780:    names the flag: the card forbids it, so do NOT route around it — end with `status: BLOCKED`.\r\n781: 2. **Your FINAL message is exactly one `result/1` JSON object** and nothing else, and the same object is written to\r\n782:    `tools/bench/cards/result_<id>.json` and checked with `py tools/protocol.py validate <that file>`:\r\n783:    `{\"schema\":\"result/1\",\"id\":\"<card id>\",\"status\":\"PASS|FAIL|BLOCKED\",\"gates\":{\"pass\":n,\"fail\":m},\r\n784:    \"first_fail\":null|\"...\",\"blocked_by\":null|{\"device\":\"...\",\"message\":\"...\"},\"artefacts\":[{\"path\",\"md5\"}],\r\n785:    \"facts\":[\"≤10, ≤200 chars each, each with file:line\"],\"open\":[\"≤3 judgement questions\"],\r\n786:    \"cost\":{\"usd\":null,\"minutes\":n,\"labview_runs\":n},\"note\":\"\"}`.\r\n787:    `blocked_by` is REQUIRED whenever a hook or gate refused the work (cycle 71 lost 57 min to a BLOCKED that no\r\n788:    fixed field reported). The prose section \"Your reply to the caller\" below is the pre-v1 shape; the JSON wins.\r\n789: 3. Every recipe / bench script you write ends with a C6 `RESULT {...}` line (`protocol.result_line(...)`; stagekit\r\n790:    prints it for you). bgrun marks a run without one `(NO RESULT LINE)` and the audit counts it.\r\n791: \r\n792: ## First, always\r\n793: \r\n794: 1. Read `CLAUDE.md` and `STATUS.md` from disk. Every rule there binds you — rule 1 (never modify an original\r\n795:    `.vi`), 1a (never change the original's computation), 1b (hardware follows the rig state in STATUS), 1c (no\r\n796:    serial on the frame path), the GUI gate, the peer/cycle gates, the bgrun discipline. Do not assume the caller\r\n797:    summarised them correctly.\r\n798: 2. Invoke the `labview-automation` skill before any LabVIEW work.\r\n799: 3. Check `tasklist | grep -i labview` and the lock block in `STATUS.md`. One COM client at a time.\r\n800: \r\n801: ## What you do\r\n802: \r\n803: - Write recipes/diagnostics as **one long script with a prediction contract in its docstring**, every name\r\n804:   resolved from `docs/NAMES.md` and `docs/toolkit-capabilities.md` before writing. Run them only through\r\n805:   `MATERIAL=1 py tools/bgrun.py --max-min N --log tools/bench/<name>.log -- py -u <script>` (Bash tool; in\r\n806:   PowerShell `$env:MATERIAL='1'; py tools\\bgrun.py ...`). **The `MATERIAL=1` prefix is mandatory** —\r\n807:   `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py` refuses any `tools/recipes/*.py` or `tools/bench/*.py` run without it, because a\r\n808:   judgement session must delegate such runs to you rather than run them itself (CLAUDE.md §3). Never patch files\r\n809:   with a heredoc; use the Edit/Write tools.\r\n810: - **Before creating any new op, tool, or recipe**, check what already exists (`docs/toolkit-capabilities.md`,\r\n811:   `grep \"^def \" tools/gscript.py`, `ls tools/recipes tools/bench`) and say in the docstring what you found.\r\n812:   Most of this project's cost has been rebuilding things it already owned.\r\n813: - When a hook blocks you (`guard_peer`, `guard_cycle`, `guard_bash`), do what it says — dispatch the peer,\r\n814:   write the disposition, run the retrospective. Never set `PEER_GUARD_OFF` / `CYCLE_GUARD_OFF`.\r\n815: - Dispatch peers only through `tools/peer.ps1` under bgrun; classify the outcome; write the\r\n816:   \"What was done with it\" section; **never attach `.vi` files**.\r\n817: - Keep `STATUS.md`'s lock and \"current state\" true **during** the work, not just at the end.\r\n818: - **STATUS.md over ~110 lines at the end of your session: relocate the narrative yourself** (rule 4; verbatim\r\n819:   into `archive/<date>-status-<topic>.md`, one line + pointer left per item). This is bookkeeping, not judgement —\r\n820:   do not leave it under OPEN (judgement, 2026-09-17, after three sessions each reported it and none did it).\r\n821: - Scratch VIs: unique name per run, created and deleted in the same run. Save only under `user.lib\\claudeDev`.\r\n822: \r\n823: ## Never end your session while something you dispatched is still running\r\n824: \r\n825: A sub-agent that \"holds until the monitor fires\" and returns has ENDED — nothing fires afterwards, and on\r\n826: 2026-09-17 that left a prior-art review running, the LabVIEW lock held, and the caller re-waking you by hand. Wait\r\n827: for your own dispatch with a bounded foreground loop that touches no LabVIEW (Bash, `timeout` up to 600000:\r\n828: `until grep -q \"BGRUN END\\|BGRUN TIMEOUT\" <log>; do sleep 10; done`), re-issue it if the deadline passes, and only\r\n829: then continue. Return only when your work is done or the failure budget is spent.\r\n830: \r\n831: ## Failure budget = 2\r\n832: \r\n833: If the same build or diagnostic fails twice, **stop**. Write the two logs' failure lines and your best two\r\n834: competing explanations into your summary and return. Do not grind a third attempt — that is the judgement\r\n835: session's call.\r\n836: \r\n837: ## After ANY failed gate, read the Jev ladder's NEXT-ACTION first — and do it\r\n838: \r\n839: The review ladder's verdict drives the next step; it does not only lift the review gate (user, 2026-09-24 03:5x,\r\n840: on \"스크립트 버그임이 Jev로 밝혀지면 판단세션에서는 그에 맞춰 동작을 바꾸는건지\"; memory principle *advisory-only is\r\n841: not delegation*). Before diagnosing, read the NEWEST `JEV-LADDER` line for that log in `tools/bench/jev_gate.log`\r\n842: (`grep \"JEV-LADDER | .* | <log name> |\" tools/bench/jev_gate.log | tail -1`) and follow its `NEXT-ACTION:` —\r\n843: this is a user-decided rule, not a result-dependent action taken in your session:\r\n844: - `patch the script and rerun; no review, no judgement turn` → fix our own file and rerun; the failure budget of 2\r\n845:   still counts the reruns.\r\n846: - `apply the cited review's disposition (<path>) and rerun` → do what that review's `## What was done with it`\r\n847:   section says; no new diagnosis.\r\n848: - `hypothesis review owed (old path)` (or no ladder line) → the old path: dispatch the review, then report.\r\n849: Report it to the caller as ONE table row in FACTS — `log | ladder class p | NEXT-ACTION | what you did | result` —\r\n850: never as a log excerpt.\r\n851: \r\n852: ## If the brief contains result-dependent actions, do not take them\r\n853: \r\n854: If the brief or the plan's `## Pre-decided` section already answers a question, apply it and say which line;\r\n855: collect any genuinely open questions into ONE `OPEN:` block at the end rather than returning one per session.\r\n856: \r\n857: A brief that says \"if the measurement shows X, then change Y\" is mis-written (CLAUDE.md §3, 2026-09-16). Run the\r\n858: measurement, report the facts, put \"brief pre-scripted: <the action>\" under `OPEN:`, and stop. The judgement\r\n859: session will decide and re-delegate. Do this even when the choice looks obvious — obviousness before the\r\n860: evidence is exactly what the rule exists to catch.\r\n861: \r\n862: ## What you do NOT decide\r\n863: \r\n864: Design changes, what to accept from a review, rule-1a equivalence calls, changing the plan's direction, patching\r\n865: more than the one thing the task named. If the task cannot be completed without one of those, do every part that\r\n866: does not depend on it, then return with the question stated in one sentence and the evidence beside it.\r\n867: \r\n868: ## Your reply to the caller — the whole point\r\n869: \r\n870: **With a CARD prompt (protocol v1): the `result/1` JSON object above, nothing else.** Only for a legacy prose brief\r\n871: (no `CARD` line) return **at most ~30 lines**, in this shape, nothing else:\r\n872: \r\n873: ```\r\n874: RESULT: <one line: done / failed / blocked-on-judgement>\r\n875: RAN: <script> -> <log>   (bgrun END|TIMEOUT line, rc)\r\n876: GATES: <n pass / m fail>; failing: <labels>\r\n877: FACTS:  - <measured fact, with the log line or file:line>\r\n878:         - ...\r\n879: CHANGED: <files edited, one line each>\r\n880: PEER:   <slug, outcome, one-line verdict>   (if any)\r\n881: OPEN:   <the one question only judgement can answer, if any>\r\n882: ```\r\n883: \r\n884: No narrative of what you tried, no restating the task, no advice unless asked. The caller will read the log\r\n885: itself only if your FACTS are insufficient — so make them sufficient.\r\nFILE: CLAUDE.md\r\n1: ---\r\n2: type: rules\r\n3: status: current\r\n4: date: 2026-09-15\r\n5: tags: [rules]\r\n6: ---\r\n7: \r\n8: # Standing rules — read before doing anything\r\n9: \r\n10: ## 1. Never modify an original file. Ever.\r\n11: \r\n12: Applies to **every** pre-existing `.vi` this project touches: the reference VI\r\n13: `Min_Track N beads 4.5_KimLabMTroom_3StateClamping.vi`, everything in `zz_LabView VI\\background\r\n14: VIs\\`, and any original opened for inspection. **Copies are always fine** — new/derived work goes in\r\n15: `C:\\Program Files\\National Instruments\\LabVIEW 2026\\user.lib\\claudeDev`, never saved over an\r\n16: original, never into this project folder.\r\n17: \r\n18: A `*` in a LabVIEW title bar does **not** mean the disk file changed — only an explicit Save does.\r\n19: If an original ends up dirty: close **without saving**, verify the on-disk checksum, continue.\r\n20: Answer **Don't Save** to every save prompt naming an original (merely *opening* a hierarchy in a\r\n21: newer LabVIEW relinks subVIs, dirties them, and one \"Save\" on exit rewrites the whole hierarchy in\r\n22: the newer format — irreversibly). After any session that opened originals, audit saved-version\r\n23: bytes offline: `19 00 80 00` = LV2019, `26 00 80 00` = LV2026.\r\n24: \r\n25: ## 1a. Never change the original's COMPUTATION — this is a behaviour-preserving refactor\r\n26: \r\n27: **User, 2026-08-30: \"원본의 연산방법 자체를 바꾸면 안돼. 이건 꼭 명심하고.\"** Only *scheduling* may\r\n28: change (shift registers → auto-indexed tunnels, parallel instances). The per-bead maths, the\r\n29: parameters that reach it, and the numbers that come out must be identical.\r\n30: \r\n31: - A change of decomposition can be a change of computation — any kernel substitution must be\r\n32:   justified as equivalent or checked numerically, never assumed.\r\n33: - Parameters must arrive by the same route with the same values; falling back to a default is a\r\n34:   computation change in disguise. Whole-array parameters must cross loop borders **non-indexed**.\r\n35: - **Acceptance is numeric**: same inputs → same X/Y/Z through old and new paths. `ExecState == 1`\r\n36:   says nothing about equivalence.\r\n37: - When a step cannot be shown computation-preserving, **stop and ask the user**.\r\n38: \r\n39: ## 1b. Hardware permission is a function of the RIG STATE — and the ASI is not a special case\r\n40: \r\n41: **RE-ISSUED 2026-09-16 by the user**, replacing every earlier hardware rule:\r\n42: *\"모터 접근 및 카메라 접근을 '리그 분해 / 리그 조립 / 실험중' 상태에 따라 다르게 두는게 맞는듯. 리그 분해 →\r\n43: 모든 모터 및 카메라 접근 허용. 리그 조립 → 카메라 접근만 허용. 실험중 → 모든 모터 및 카메라 접근 불가.\r\n44: 이는 Piezo stage인 ASI 컨트롤러를 포함하는 내용 (ASI와 다른 모터를 구분하여 권한 두지 말것).\"*\r\n45: \r\n46: | rig state | motors (PI translation · rotor · magnet) | **ASI piezo stage** | camera |\r\n47: |---|---|---|---|\r\n48: | **분해 — disassembled** | ✅ allowed | ✅ **allowed, exactly like any other motor** | ✅ allowed |\r\n49: | **조립 — assembled** | ⚠️ **ONLY through `tools/motor_gate.py`, inside the safe-motion envelope** | ⚠️ same gateway, same envelope | ✅ allowed |\r\n50: | **실험중 — experiment running** | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |\r\n51: \r\n52: - **The 조립 row was ❌ ❌ until the user amended it on 2026-09-17 evening** (*\"조립 상태에서도 이 범위 안이면 모터\r\n53:   허용함\"*, after *\"실험 1차로 끝났는데, 리그는 유지되는 중\"*). The later statement NARROWS rather than widens: motors\r\n54:   and the ASI are reachable while assembled **only** through the single gateway `tools/motor_gate.py` and only\r\n55:   inside the safe-motion envelope (PI magnet 0–39 mm · ASI x/y inside the `SL`/`SU` limits the user set AT THE RIG on\r\n56:   2026-09-18 15:2x — live position **±2 mm**, never homed · ASI z free). ⚠️ **The numbers live in `STATUS.md`'s\r\n57:   hardware banner and in `tools/bench/motor_limits.json`; never restate them here.** This line said \"≤ ~1 mm from the\r\n58:   anchor\" until 2026-09-19, when `doc_ingest` measured it against the banner's absolute `SL`/`SU` values (a 4.0 mm-wide\r\n59:   window in each axis) and the cycle-41 judgement session resolved it the same way rule 1b was resolved on 2026-09-18:\r\n60:   the later user statement wins and STATUS was the one that was right,\r\n61:   **conditional on that envelope being really enforced as refusing code**. The envelope says what is SAFE, not when\r\n62:   motors are allowed; the rig state still decides that. Numbers and the gate's live-move evidence:\r\n63:   `STATUS.md` hardware banner. Resolved 2026-09-18 by the cycle-18 judgement session after `doc_ingest` reported\r\n64:   this row contradicting STATUS.md — later user statement wins, and STATUS was the one that was right.\r\n65: - **Never give the ASI its own rule again.** The old carve-out (\"the one instrument that can physically break the\r\n66:   rig\", the 2026-09-13 permission conditioned on the piezo being *detached*) is **retired**. Permission is decided\r\n67:   by the rig's state, not by the instrument's identity — when the rig is apart there is nothing to collide with.\r\n68: - **CONTROLLER LIMITS ARE SET AT EVERY CYCLE START AND RELEASED AT EVERY CYCLE END, BY THE RUNNER, EACH VERIFIED BY READBACK** (user, 2026-09-23: \"훅으로 묶어서 매 사이클마다 시작할때는 묶고, 종료시에는 풀고. 그 다음 각 싸이클 시작 및 종료 시점마다 제대로 리밋 셋팅 되어있는지 확인하도록\"). `cycle_runner.py` `motor_limits_hook` runs `motor_gate.py --session start` before and `--session end` after every cycle; a start that does not verify stops the runner before the cycle, an end that does not verify stops it after and writes STATUS. In 실험중/unknown state nothing is sent and the skip is logged. **SESSION START = A REAL REFERENCE MOVE FIRST, THEN A COMMANDED-vs-READBACK CHECK, RETRIED UP TO 3× (user, 2026-09-23 after the near-miss: \"반드시 싸이클 시작할 때는 PI 모터 원점 복귀 반드시 시킨 후에 실제 좌표와 아웃풋 좌표 비교하는거 반드시 만들어\" · \"원점 복귀하여 복원 시키고 사이클 시작한다 … 여러 차례 복원 시도해도 문제가 생긴다면 그 때는 사이클 종료\"):** `motor_send_pi.ps1` limits-set now sends `SVO 1 1`, `RON 1 1`, `FNL 1` (0 = negative limit switch), requires `FRF? 1` and `POS 0`, then moves 2 mm and back and compares readback to the command within 0.05 mm; `motor_gate.session_start` retries the whole PI step `REF_ATTEMPTS`=3 times and refuses only after all fail, which is when the runner does not start the cycle. The old \"reference restore\" (`RON 1 0` + `POS 1 <current>`) is GONE: after a controller power-cycle the counter reads 0 wherever the stage physically is, and declaring that zero drove the magnet into the hard limit on 2026-09-23 (`tools/bench/pi_testmove_20260923e.log`, ERR 216). Why: `--session start` was run once on 2026-09-18 and `--session end` never, so the limits sat on the controllers for five days and had to be released by hand before an experiment (`tools/bench/motor_session_end_20260923.log`).\r\n69: - **MOTOR GRANT 2026-09-24 (user: \"당분간 내가 말하기 전까지는 모터 접속 허용함. 다만 원점 확인 및 모터 리밋, 두 가지는 꼭 확인 필요\"):** while it stands, motors may be driven by the gate and by a RUNNING MAIN VI with the rig assembled — a newly assembled main VI's acceptance test IS a real run (\"이제 메인 vi 조립시에는 실제 작동시켜야 할테니 그대로 해볼 것\"); recorded-frame replay is used only when a real run cannot answer. The two checks are never skipped (session-start reference + verify, limits set/released with readback). **EVERY CYCLE ENDS WITH LabVIEW CLOSED AND VERIFIED GONE** (\"사이클 종료하고서는 제대로 LabVIEW 끄는것 잊지 말것, 특히 카메라가 계속 Acquisition 하면 기계에 좋지 않으니\") — `cycle_runner.py` end hook, mechanical. The grant ends only when the user says so.\r\n70: - **Only the user announces a state change**, and the announcement is the boundary. Never infer a transition from\r\n71:   silence, from a quiet period, or from how long a session has run.\r\n72: - **Do not ask per incident** inside a state the user has already declared (*\"Don't need to hastle around me\r\n73:   before I tell you.\"*). Re-requesting permission each session is the error.\r\n74: - While **disassembled**, hardware measurement is expected work — true VISA/serial latency, the per-frame cost of\r\n75:   motor reading, the rotor negative-angle check on `SetCommand_signed.vi`. It is cheapest now and expensive after\r\n76:   reassembly, so it is not deferred to \"a supervised session\".\r\n77: - **The rotor is SIGNED — the new VI uses `SetCommand_signed.vi` and follows the hardware number, sign included**\r\n78:   (user, 2026-09-16: *\"로터는 본래 부호 인식이 가능했으나 랩뷰 시리얼 통신에서 부호 인식이 안되는 문제가 있었음.\r\n79:   이제는 해결 방법을 찾은 것 같으니 부호를 포함하여 하드웨어 숫자 그대로 따라가야함.\"*). The unsigned\r\n80:   `SetCommand.vi` was a workaround for a serial-path defect, not the rotor's nature. \"해결 방법을 찾은 것 **같으니**\"\r\n81:   — the sign round-trip on the real link is still to be measured while disassembled, not assumed.\r\n82: - Superseded and not to be reinstated from an old summary: the 2026-08-27 blanket motor ban\r\n83:   (*\"우선 모터 가동은 절대 하지 않는 선에서 계속 처리해\"*) and the piezo-detached condition.\r\n84: \r\n85: The realistic hazard remains a VI run as a side effect: LabVIEW's broken-arrow and Run buttons are the same\r\n86: pixels — check the arrow state or use menus before clicking that toolbar region on any VI that can reach an\r\n87: instrument.\r\n88: \r\n89: ## 1c. No serial on the frame path — the user's data-quality constraint (2026-09-16)\r\n90: \r\n91: *\"I don't want to have even a single frame loss coming from the motor communication if possible. And it is so\r\n92: clear that serial communication through VISA can somehow stall the loop, and cause unwanted frame stop.\"* And the\r\n93: fact behind it: the sample stage **drifts on its own** (thermal drift, the sample-holder pin shifting, causes the\r\n94: user cannot name), so **focus re-adjustment is continuous, not rare** — \"I can't tell how frequent it is, but it\r\n95: is not trivial.\"\r\n96: \r\n97: So the constraint is structural, not statistical: **no VISA/serial call may sit anywhere on the frame acquisition\r\n98: path.** A mechanism that *can* stall the frame loop is disqualified even if it usually does not. Serial lives in\r\n99: its own loop, owns its VISA session exclusively, and reaches the frame path only through a non-blocking handoff.\r\n100: Any argument of the form \"the serial branch is conditional, so it is cheap on average\" is refuted at the premise.\r\n101: \r\n102: ## 1c''. Loop-to-loop CONTROL signals travel by LOCAL VARIABLE (latest value), never by queue; the focus loop runs on its own clock (user, 2026-09-25)\r\n103: \r\n104: *\"큐를 넣어버린다면 두 루프 사이에 상관관계가 생겨버린다 … 그런 리스크를 감당할 필요가 있는지 모르겠음. 그냥 Boolean 값 및\r\n105: 타겟 값을 local variable로 전달하는게 더 좋지 않을지? ASI autofocus 루프는 애초에 frame acquisition 루프와 동시에 돌\r\n106: 필요가 없을듯.\"* A queue couples producer and consumer (backlog, timeouts, shutdown order); a local variable does not —\r\n107: the writer never waits and the reader sees the latest value. So: **control / trigger signals between loops (focus\r\n108: requests, setpoints, enable flags, counters) are locals; queues are for lossless DATA streams only** (the file writer's\r\n109: results FIFO, master plan 1.7). **Never detect an EDGE on a polled boolean** (that is what produced STATUS OPEN 58's\r\n110: three autofocus limits); publish a value the reader can compare (a counter, a position).\r\n111: \r\n112: **Autofocus — the user's account (2026-09-25) and what the code MEASURES:** the user remembers a distance-threshold\r\n113: trigger on the **first-clicked reference bead**; the offline read of Case `#10407` (`docs/autofocus-case-10407.md`,\r\n114: card chat-F1) shows NO threshold: every firing (every 25 frames, `camera-acquisition-facts.md:255-270`) moves the axis\r\n115: by `clamp(idx_bead0 − slices/2 + 'Focus Deviation from the Center', ±0.2)` — a proportional correction with an\r\n116: additive setpoint OFFSET, on bead index 0; `Focus Step (F1)` is not read there; the `In Range?` node is unwired.\r\n117: **Rule 1a decides: the redesign copies the CODE, not the memory.** A threshold trigger would be a new feature and\r\n118: needs the user's separate decision. The user does not run with `Frame rate` = 1. DESIGN IN FORCE for the focus loop\r\n119: (after M3, `docs/connectivity-map-plan.md` Pre-decided 190 — renumbered 2026-09-25 from 148, which clashed with `d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md`): frame loop publishes the reference bead's z and the\r\n120: auto-reset counter as locals; the focus loop runs on its own time cadence, reads them, applies the original's\r\n121: correction arithmetic (offset, clamp ±0.2, bead index 0) under `Auto-Focus` / `Limit of Auto-Focus`, and moves — no\r\n122: schedule boolean in the frame loop, no edge detection, no queue. Scheduling changes; the arithmetic does not (rule 1a).\r\n123: Mechanical: `tools/stage_prerun.py` refuses a stage plan that creates a queue primitive for a control signal or\r\n124: wires a boolean into a shift-register edge detector across loops (`control_path_lint`).\r\n125: \r\n126: ## 1c'. Test runs are UNATTENDED until the rig is reassembled — the harness clicks the beads itself (user, 2026-09-17)\r\n127: \r\n128: The main VI runs as: panel parameters → device configure → **a while loop that waits for mouse clicks on the live\r\n129: image (first = reference bead, rest = magnetic beads)** → **a button** that ends it and runs bead-profile\r\n130: calibration → the experiment loop. Nobody present ⇒ it stalls in the picking loop. The user's requirement:\r\n131: *\"리그 조립 전까지는 내가 없는 환경에서 돌리기를 기대함 … 몇십번이고 돌려야 할테니 … 밤 시간동안 하네스 루프\r\n132: 돌리며 개발 하기를 기대함.\"* Their choice of mechanism, **option 1**: *\"직접 마우스 움직여서 디스플레이 상에 클릭\r\n133: 이후 버튼 누르기 → 저장 위치 및 저장 이름 쓰기\"* — i.e. scripted GUI clicks on the image display, the done\r\n134: button, and the save-path entry, through `tools/lv_gui.ps1` with `-Exception Approved -Evidence \"user 2026-09-17\r\n135: bead-pick option 1\"`. A `.cal`-loading substitute VI (option 2) was **rejected**. *\"Bead가 없는 상황에서 트래킹\r\n136: 에러가 분명히 생기겠으나, 디바이스 통신 및 프레임 체크 용도로는 무리 없을 듯\"* — garbage tracking output in a\r\n137: no-bead run is not a failure. **GPU path first; CPU afterwards** (re-affirmed the same day).\r\n138: \r\n139: ## 1d. The main VI is in scope — read-only, and read it when meaning requires it\r\n140: \r\n141: (User, 2026-08-30: examine the main VI when the situation calls for it; effort belongs on\r\n142: *semantic* analysis.) Never modify, never save; prefer **headless COM reads** over editor windows.\r\n143: **Read nested structures properly** — a flat strings dump once made calibration-cluster *fields*\r\n144: look like ignorable stray controls.\r\n145: \r\n146: ## 2. LabVIEW is shared with real experiments — be interruptible at any point\r\n147: \r\n148: The user may forcibly stop a session at any moment to reclaim LabVIEW. Keep `STATUS.md`'s state\r\n149: current **during** work — after each real milestone, not just at session end. Treat \"the user might\r\n150: vanish right now\" as the normal case.\r\n151: \r\n152: ### 2b. The user's messages during work are interrupts — answer first, then continue\r\n153: \r\n154: (User, 2026-08-27; re-issued 2026-08-28 after a compaction let it fade; re-issued 2026-09-04\r\n155: \"전체 세션을 돌더라도 항상 응답 즉각적으로 할 수 있게 규칙 잡지 않았었나?\".) A user message that\r\n156: arrives mid-turn is answered in the **very next reply, first line, before any tool call** — a\r\n157: progress line (\"지금 ~ 확인 중\") is not an answer, and a yes/no question gets its yes/no in the first\r\n158: sentence, explanation after. Stop or redirect the running work if that is what the message implies.\r\n159: Background anything slow; prefer short checkable steps to long chains. Re-read this whenever the\r\n160: session is summarized.\r\n161: \r\n162: ## 2c. Once a cycle is turning, RUN IT TO THE END — do not pause it to ask (user, 2026-09-16)\r\n163: \r\n164: *\"한 번 루프 돌기 시작했을 때, 꼭 필요하지 않다면 중간에 멈추지 않고 끝까지 루프 계속 진행했으면 좋겠음.\r\n165: 규약에도 갱신하면 좋을듯.\"* Said after a session stopped mid-cycle to put a genuine question to the user — a gate\r\n166: was blocking the build over a date-comparison bug — while other work in the same cycle was still runnable.\r\n167: \r\n168: **The default is: finish the cycle, then report once.** When something is uncertain mid-cycle: (1) do every part\r\n169: that does not depend on the answer, all of it; (2) for the part that does, **write the assumption down, proceed\r\n170: under it**, and flag it in the closing report so the user overturns one thing rather than unblocks ten; (3) stop\r\n171: only when proceeding under *any* assumption would be unsafe, destructive, or would make the work useless if wrong.\r\n172: \r\n173: This is about **where the waiting happens**. A question asked mid-cycle makes the user the scheduler; the same\r\n174: question in the closing report lets them answer whenever, with the finished work already in front of them. It does\r\n175: not weaken 2b — their messages arriving mid-work are still answered in the very next reply, first line. Claude\r\n176: stays responsive to the user; the user does not become responsible for keeping the cycle moving.\r\n177: \r\n178: ## 3. Autonomous progress protocol\r\n179: \r\n180: **Start/stop is explicit, never inferred** — the user says start (\"지금 진행해\") and stop (\"멈춰\");\r\n181: silence is NOT authorization. Maintain the lock block in `STATUS.md` (acquired ↔ released) so any\r\n182: future session can tell at a glance whether LabVIEW is safe to touch:\r\n183: \r\n184: ```yaml\r\n185: labview-lock:\r\n186:   status: released   # acquired | released\r\n187:   owner:\r\n188:   since:\r\n189:   purpose:\r\n190: ```\r\n191: \r\n192: - **GUI only where scripting is VERIFIED unreachable** (user, 2026-08-31: \"GUI 활용은 script로\r\n193:   도달 불가능한 경우에만\"). **Mechanically enforced**: `lv_gui.ps1` refuses state-changing actions\r\n194:   (click/rclick/dclick/drag/wire/keys) without `-Exception VerifiedImpossible|NegativeSearch|Approved`\r\n195:   plus `-Evidence <record>`, and logs every authorized one to `tools/gui_actions.log`. The\r\n196:   verified-impossible list (skill; currently 2 entries) names operation CLASSES — each concrete use\r\n197:   must also show no scriptable alternative construction exists. Cost/time is never a reason. Known\r\n198:   laundering patterns to refuse in review: a too-narrow \"negative search\", \"only opening it to\r\n199:   look\", and plan clauses like \"via GUI if needed\" (every GUI act must be individually enumerated\r\n200:   in the peer-reviewed plan). One execution path at a time.\r\n201: - **Failed batch ⇒ RECOVERY_LOCKED** (peer-reviewed design, 2026-08-31,\r\n202:   `archive/peer/...gui-rule-hole-analysis-review.md`): when a scripted batch hits an unpredicted\r\n203:   result, state-changing GUI is OFF; only diagnostics (screenshots, window lists, reporter reads,\r\n204:   Esc) are allowed. Produce a recovery packet (prediction, observation, evidence, competing\r\n205:   explanation, cheapest discriminating test), get a **successful** peer review (a timeout/quota\r\n206:   failure is not a review), then resume as a NEW batch — never by continuing the failed one\r\n207:   interactively. Recipes must state their prediction contracts (expected counts/states/tolerances)\r\n208:   before execution so \"failed prediction\" is machine-checkable.\r\n209: - **Save authority**: files under `user.lib\\claudeDev` may be saved autonomously. Never originals.\r\n210: - **Restart authority** (user, 2026-08-29: \"재시작 항상 오케이\"): restart/kill LabVIEW when it is\r\n211:   the right fix, no per-incident approval; save valuables to disk first.\r\n212: \r\n213: ### Reference hygiene — close every reference, and PROVE it by handle count (user, 2026-09-06)\r\n214: \r\n215: (\"이후 close reference 필수로 사용하도록 하고, 규칙에 확실히 규정할 것. 그리고 해당 규칙이 반영되는지\r\n216: 검증.\" — after LabVIEW turned sluggish/'응답 없음' at 32,480 handles following ~30 h of scripting.)\r\n217: \r\n218: 1. **Every VI Server reference is closed by whoever opened it**: op VIs wire `Close Reference`\r\n219:    on every ref they create (Open VI Reference, Traverse arrays, Get Outputs, New VI Object,\r\n220:    creator outputs); Python releases cached VI references on exit and never abandons a COM call\r\n221:    (a killed or hung client keeps its references alive inside LabVIEW).\r\n222: 2. **Measured, not assumed**: `tools/bench/handle_audit.py` attributes handle growth to operation\r\n223:    types; `bench_prep.py` reads LabVIEW's handle count before every batch and restarts the\r\n224:    instance above `HANDLE_LIMIT` (mechanical, standing restart permission). A new op is accepted\r\n225:    only if 20 runs leave the handle count flat (±100). **Baseline: this LabVIEW 2026 install holds\r\n226:    ~31,500 handles one minute after a fresh start** (measured 2026-09-06 04:16) — the first\r\n227:    \"32,480 = leak\" diagnosis was WRONG; judge growth relative to that baseline, never the absolute\r\n228:    number. Responsiveness itself is measured directly with `lv_gui.ps1 -Action ping`\r\n229:    (SendMessageTimeout latency per window: the metric behind '응답 없음').\r\n230: 3. Any batch that must kill a client (deadline) is followed by a handle read; a jump is logged as\r\n231:    a non-result and the instance restarted before the next batch.\r\n232: \r\n233: ### Usage-limit protocol (user, 2026-09-04)\r\n234: \r\n235: (\"만약 중간에 이용한도가 꽉 차면 (95% 초과할 것 같으면) 리뉴얼 시점보다 2분 이후로 일정 예약\r\n236: 걸어두고 하던 작업 이어가도록. 벤치 중간에 멈췄다면 해당 벤치는 다시 돌리도록.\")\r\n237: \r\n238: 1. **Detect.** Before each long unit of work (a benchmark cell, a batch build) check the usage\r\n239:    readout if one is available; a usage/rate-limit error is the reactive trigger. Never assume the\r\n240:    quota is fine because nothing complained.\r\n241: 2. **Schedule, don't wait.** At ~95 % or on a limit error, read the renewal time from the limit\r\n242:    message and schedule a wake-up at **renewal + 2 min** (session wake-up for ≤ 1 h, a scheduled\r\n243:    task otherwise). Write the resume point to `STATUS.md` first. Do not end the turn with \"resume\r\n244:    me later\" — the schedule is the resume.\r\n245: 3. **Rerun, don't resume.** A benchmark cell or build interrupted mid-run is invalid: after the\r\n246:    wake-up it is rerun **from the beginning** and the partial attempt is logged as a non-result\r\n247:    (which cell, when, why). Half-run numbers never enter a results table.\r\n248: 4. **Never wait on a user turn** for something a script can do (registering definitions, restarting\r\n249:    a tool, re-queuing a cell). If a route needs the user, say that in one sentence and build the\r\n250:    scripted route.\r\n251: \r\n252: ### Reports reach the user THROUGH THE CLAUDE SESSION — the user is remote (user, 2026-09-25)\r\n253: \r\n254: *\"보통은 내가 원격으로 러너 확인 및 지침을 줌. 그러니 클로드 세션으로 확인 필요함. 훅 경로 변경하도록.\"* A Windows toast,\r\n255: a local file or a session-only timer (CronCreate, ScheduleWakeup — both failed silently, 09-23 and 09-25) is not a\r\n256: report. Path in force: (1) `cycle_runner.py` writes `HEARTBEAT` at every cycle end and at RUNNER STOP\r\n257: (`tools/bench/heartbeat_latest.md`, mechanical, no model); (2) the app-level scheduled task `runner-cycle-report`\r\n258: (`~/.claude/scheduled-tasks/runner-cycle-report/SKILL.md`, every 30 min, survives session changes, runs while the app\r\n259: is open) turns new events into a Korean report (`tools/bench/reports/`), acks `report_gate`, and its completion\r\n260: notifies the main chat session, which relays the report verbatim and calls `PushNotification` (phone when mobile\r\n261: push is on); (3) `tools/hooks/report_gate.py` still blocks the chat's turn on unreported events when the user speaks\r\n262: first. Open user decisions (`tools/bench/decisions_pending.json`) are part of every report.\r\n263: \r\n264: ### Unattended runs: silence is not progress (2026-09-05) — now mechanical\r\n265: \r\n266: A log monitor only reports what a live process writes. Two mechanisms replace attention:\r\n267: (1) `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py` refuses any backgrounded LabVIEW command unless it runs through\r\n268: `py tools/bgrun.py --max-min N --log <file> -- <cmd>`, which kills the process tree at the deadline\r\n269: and always writes a final `BGRUN END|TIMEOUT` line; (2) gscript's `_run/_invoke` watchdog caps\r\n270: Run/Invoke calls; an attempt to route GetVIReference/SetControlValue through the same thread\r\n271: (`tools/gscript.py.guarded_attempt_20260905`) itself blocked in cross-apartment marshalling and was\r\n272: rolled back — the PROCESS-level deadline of bgrun is the guarantee, not per-call guards. (User, 2026-09-05: \"Timeout 검사에서 우회된 내용이 있는 것 같은데 규칙 개선\r\n273: 필요할듯\", after a background job hung 3 h 48 min.) Original prose rule kept below for the why.\r\n274: \r\n275: A log monitor only reports what a live process writes; a driver that dies writes nothing, and the\r\n276: session sat 2.5 h believing a benchmark was running. While anything unattended runs, schedule a\r\n277: heartbeat (ScheduleWakeup / cron, ≤ 30 min) that checks the process list AND the log's age, and\r\n278: treat \"no new line for longer than one cell\" as a failure to diagnose, not as waiting.\r\n279: \r\n280: ### Usage discipline — fewer turns, one cycle per session (user, 2026-09-14: \"턴수 줄이는것 해보고 주기적 컨텍스트 정리도 적용해보자\")\r\n281: \r\n282: The user found Fable usage burning \"말도 안되게 빠른\" after a 13-hour session. The burn is **turns × context**: every\r\n283: background notification is a full turn over the whole conversation. Standing rules:\r\n284: \r\n285: 1. **One LabVIEW batch = one runner = one notification.** Chain build → test → archive/INDEX bookkeeping into a single\r\n286:    Python runner under one `bgrun` (as `build_opqueue_all.py` does); never a build turn, then a test turn, then a doc\r\n287:    turn. Dispatch the mandatory peer review AND prepare the fix in the same turn; read logs with a targeted grep, not\r\n288:    whole files; never re-read what is already in context.\r\n289: 2. **Session = one cycle.** Start a session by reading STATUS.md and the cycle's plan document only; end it by writing\r\n290:    STATUS's \"Next\" line and stop. Do not carry a second cycle in the same session — start a fresh one (the layered docs\r\n291:    exist for exactly this cold start; a fresh session beats compacting a long one).\r\n292:    **ENFORCED BY A RUNNER — user decision 2026-09-17 (\"2번으로 가자. Opus max\"), after a 15-hour session:** cycles are\r\n293:    not started by a person and not continued in a chat. `tools/cycle_runner.py` loops: spawn a fresh `claude -p`\r\n294:    judgement session (**Opus, effort max** — not Fable; **AMENDED 2026-09-23 by the user: `claude-opus-5-5` pinned by id at effort HIGH** — the Artificial Analysis index shows high→max = +4 points at 3.3× cost (news.hada.io/topic?id=34142); hypothesis reviews max→high, priorart 5.5/medium, material 5.5/medium, log-reader 5.5/low — **re-set 14:xx to the table the user approved (\"오케이 테이블대로 반영하자\") after the measured fact that Opus 5.5's effort scale differs: its medium scores as Opus 5's max on the Artificial Analysis index and is its default (platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort); judgement = medium**; the alias `opus` resolved to `claude-opus-5` and is no longer used, so a later alias change cannot silently move the comparison baseline; needs Claude Code ≥ 2.1.280) that reads STATUS.md + the current plan only, runs ONE cycle\r\n295:    (delegate → decide → retrospective → STATUS NEXT), exits; the runner checks the exit and NEXT and spawns the next.\r\n296:    Runner stop conditions: a `STOP` marker in STATUS.md (written by the user), the same failure two cycles running,\r\n297:    the usage-limit rule (renewal + 2 min). `tools/hooks/guard_session.py` (PreToolUse Agent) refuses material\r\n298:    dispatches after the retrospective has run in that session and above 8 dispatches per session. **The interactive\r\n299:    chat is for talking with the user only** — it answers from STATUS and the latest retrospective and redirects the\r\n300:    runner by editing STATUS NEXT/STOP; it never runs a cycle itself. Every cycle plan carries a `## Pre-decided`\r\n301:    section (decisions material sessions apply without asking; `doc_lint` warns when it is missing).\r\n302:    **FIREFIGHTER cycles (user, 2026-09-18: \"기존 구조로 처리가 잘 안되는 부분은 Fable, low로 소방수 파견\" · \"단순히\r\n303:    판단만으로는 부족\" · \"트리거링 걸리면 발동하도록\" · \"Low로 첫번, 그 다음 middle로. 그래도 안되면 나한테 판단\r\n304:    요청\").** The runner itself decides, from bgrun logs, never a session or a person: when the SAME recipe\r\n305:    (`tools/recipes/<name>.py`, in command position) ends `BGRUN END rc≠0` / `TIMEOUT` in TWO CONSECUTIVE cycles,\r\n306:    — **or the SAME MISTAKE under another name** (user, 2026-09-18: \"동일 실수 반복하는 것도 판단 조건에 들어가야\"):\r\n307:    the same first failing GATE line (uids stripped) in two consecutive cycles even if the recipe was renamed\r\n308:    v1 → v2, or a retrospective archived in the cycle carrying `VIOLATION: repeated-failure-class` —\r\n309:    the NEXT cycle runs as a whole cycle on **fable / low** (so it can execute, not only advise; it may patch and\r\n310:    run the recipe itself with `MATERIAL=1`). If the same recipe fails again → the next cycle is **fable / medium**;\r\n311:    if it fails a third time → `RUNNER STOP`, the user's judgement is requested; there is never a third\r\n312:    firefighter. Every other rule (1, 1a, 1b, no new process device, peer review, retrospective) holds inside a\r\n313:    firefighter cycle. The user may also order one directly (`--firefighter <recipe>`, first cycle only, as on\r\n314:    2026-09-18 12:59). Self-test: `tools/bench/selftest_cycle_runner_ff.py` (2/2).\r\n315:    **A DEADLINE ENDS THE RUNNER BETWEEN CYCLES, NEVER MID-CYCLE (user, 2026-09-21: \"그냥 셧다운 하지 말고 진행 작업들 마무리하고 종료하는 방향으로. 그래야 다음 싸이클에 정상적으로 작동하지\").** `cycle_runner.py --budget-min` (default 480) is checked only between cycles; the cycle in progress always finishes (retrospective landed, NEXT written, git committed). The `bgrun --max-min` around the runner is the LAST RESORT and is set well above budget + the longest cycle (≈3 h), e.g. `--max-min 720`. A hard kill mid-cycle left a failing log with no review and deadlocked the next launch on 2026-09-21.\r\n316:    **`## NEXT` IS WRITTEN BEFORE THE RETROSPECTIVE IS LAUNCHED — MECHANICAL (user, 2026-09-21: \"NEXT 작성하도록 훅에 강제할 필요성 있을듯\").** The runner snapshots the NEXT section's md5 before spawning a session (`tools/bench/next_snapshot.md5`); `guard_bash.py next_gate()` refuses `retrospective.py` while NEXT still hashes the same. Sessions 58/64/65/66 exited waiting on their retrospective with NEXT unwritten, and the chat rewrote it four times.\r\n317: 3. **Split sessions by JUDGEMENT vs MATERIAL — ADOPTED 2026-09-15** (user, after comparing token costs directly:\r\n318:    *\"정말 필수적으로 고차원적인 판단이 필요한 경우에만 Fable 사용하고 Fable이 판단할 재료들은 opus 혹은 하위 모델로\r\n319:    세션을 잡는게 거의 필수처럼 보이는데?\"* — Opus at high effort is far cheaper than Fable at low). Note this INVERTS\r\n320:    the direction sketched here earlier: the scarce model is spent ONLY on judgement, and Opus is the\r\n321:    **material-preparation** tier, not a lower one — that is where most wall-clock time goes.\r\n322:    - **MATERIAL** (its own session, Opus high by default): measuring API facts (property short-name censuses, class\r\n323:      hierarchy, a VI's terminal names), writing/running recipes that follow an already-verified pattern, log greps,\r\n324:      INDEX/NAMES/STATUS bookkeeping, dispatching peer reviews and collecting their answers, and extracting the bare\r\n325:      facts from a failed run (which gate, which values).\r\n326:    - **JUDGEMENT** (the scarce model; Opus max while the Fable budget is out): design decisions, what to accept from a\r\n327:      review, rule-1a calls, diagnosis when hypotheses genuinely diverge, and designing the discriminating experiment.\r\n328:    - **Failure budget = 2.** A material session that fails twice STOPS, writes the logs and state down, and hands the\r\n329:      problem to a judgement session. (`OpCaseFrames_v0` looked closed-spec and failed five times — grinding is more\r\n330:      expensive than the judgement it avoids.)\r\n331:    - **Judgement sessions stay SHORT**: STATUS.md, the relevant plan document, and a summary of the failing log —\r\n332:      nothing else. A judgement session at 78 % of its context window defeats the whole point.\r\n333:    - **RE-ISSUED 2026-09-16, after the subscription was upgraded — the upgrade does NOT relax this:** *\"Fable의\r\n334:      사용량을 최대한 줄이고, 필요하다면 하부 세션을 늘려서라도 opus 비중을 높이는 게 좋음.\"* Minimise Fable\r\n335:      unconditionally; **spawn more Opus sub-sessions rather than do material work in a Fable session.** The moment\r\n336:      a judgement session is about to write a recipe, read a long log or patch a wrapper, hand off. (Trigger: on\r\n337:      2026-09-16 one Fable session did an entire cycle — recipes, log reads, a 26-wrapper patch — in one context.)\r\n338:    - **The hand-off is mechanical — two agent definitions (user, 2026-09-16 \"에이전트 정의 먼저\"):**\r\n339:      `.claude/agents/material.md` (Opus high: writes/runs recipes and diagnostics, patches tools, dispatches\r\n340:      peers, keeps STATUS; returns a ≤30-line fact summary; failure budget 2) and `.claude/agents/log-reader.md`\r\n341:      (Opus low, read-only: which gate, which values, which line; ≤25 lines). **A judgement session spawns these\r\n342:      via the Agent tool and never runs `tools/recipes/*.py` or `tools/bench/*.py` itself, never reads a whole\r\n343:      log itself.** The judgement session's turns are three kinds only: read STATUS + plan, decide, delegate.\r\n344:    - **A delegation brief states the MEASUREMENT, never the result-dependent ACTION** (2026-09-16, after the\r\n345:      user asked whether a judgement session fed ≤30-line summaries can still judge). Writing \"if A2 removes 1\r\n346:      do X, else do Y\" into a material brief is not delegation — it moves the decision into the session that\r\n347:      is not supposed to make it, before the evidence exists. The material session measures, returns FACTS and\r\n348:      an `OPEN:` line, and stops; the judgement session decides and delegates again. One extra sub-session is\r\n349:      the intended cost. The retrospective asks, per cycle, whether any decision was taken inside a material\r\n350:      session that belonged to judgement — slug `judgement-in-material`, counted by `violations.py` like the\r\n351:      others. The judgement session may always ask `log-reader` for more; ≤30 lines is a default, not a cap.\r\n352:    - **Reports the user reads are WRITTEN BY CODEX, not by Claude** (user, 2026-09-16: *\"보고는 검수를 받는게\r\n353:      아니라 그냥 chatgpt cli에 의존하는게 좋을 것 같은데? 그냥 클로드와 코덱스는 문체가 달라.\"*). For any\r\n354:      paragraph-length report, summary or decision request, Claude hands `.claude/agents/reporter.md` a **fact\r\n355:      list** (numbers, paths, gate outcomes, the question) and shows that text as-is (`peer.ps1 -Kind prose`,\r\n356:      archived under `archive/prose/`, invisible to every gate). ⚠️ **From 2026-09-18 the writer is `claude\r\n357:      -Role prose` = fable/low with a THIN prompt (`--safe-mode`), not codex** (§5, \"Codex's roles move to Claude\r\n358:      sub-sessions\"): the rule exists so the report is not in this session's sentence shapes, and a cell that never\r\n359:      loads this project's rules or context is not this session. Codex stays at `-Agent codex -Kind prose`.\r\n360:      **The dispatch is run by the judgement session\r\n361:      itself, in the FOREGROUND, not by a sub-agent** (2026-09-17, user: \"어떻게 하면 같은 일을 막을 수 있을까\"):\r\n362:      twice a `reporter` sub-agent backgrounded the call and returned, and its exit killed the child before codex\r\n363:      had written a line. The relay is one file write and one ~60 s wait; `guard_bash.py` allows exactly that\r\n364:      shape (`-Kind prose` under bgrun, foreground timeout ≤ 6 min), and it costs no sub-agent tokens. Claude still writes one-line answers (rule 2b),\r\n365:      short status lines and questions of a sentence or two. A proofreading variant was tried first and\r\n366:      rejected the same day — rewriting Claude's sentences keeps Claude's sentence shapes.\r\n367: \r\n368: ### MODEL LADDERS, decided by the runner from files (user, 2026-09-26)\r\n369: \r\n370: *\"지금 3일 안에 사용량 다 써야하는데, 당분간 Fable 5.1 low 사용빈도를 높여보는 건\"* · *\"판정 세션은 필요에 따라서 medium\r\n371: 상위 혹은 fable로 교체하는 것도 생각해보는게 어떨지\"*. Two ladders, both mechanical (`tools/cycle_runner.py`), nobody\r\n372: \"feels\" a level:\r\n373: - **Material sessions:** default **Fable 5.1 low until 2026-09-28 07:00 KST** (the weekly renewal; a one-time app\r\n374:   reminder `revert-material-model-monday` fires then), escalation rungs Fable medium → `decisions_pending`. After\r\n375:   Monday the default returns to `claude-opus-5-5` medium unless the Fable-vs-Opus comparison (result cards carry\r\n376:   `escalation`) says otherwise. Data so far: fable/low delivered L7-1b after two Opus failures (cycles 71/72); a\r\n377:   fable/low JUDGEMENT cycle (87) produced nothing in 6 min.\r\n378: - **Judgement sessions:** level 0 `claude-opus-5-5` medium → 1 Opus high → 2 Fable low → 3 Fable medium → RUNNER STOP\r\n379:   + decision item. Up one level when the last cycle left `next.json` UNCHANGED or its retrospective named a\r\n380:   judgement fault (`inference-over-measurement`, `wrong-ordering`, `judgement-in-material`); back to 0 after a cycle\r\n381:   that delivers. The recipe firefighter (fable/low on a repeated recipe failure) stays; the two never stack above\r\n382:   Fable medium. The level and its reason are a `JUDGE-LADDER` runner-log line and a field of the cycle card.\r\n383: - **Judgement effort A/B until Monday** (user, 2026-09-26: *\"Opus 5.5도 기본을 medium이 좋을지 high가 좋을지도 판단\r\n384:   필요\"*): no cycle so far ran judgement at high, so there is no basis to choose; `cycle_runner --judge-ab` alternates\r\n385:   level-0 effort by cycle parity (odd medium, even high, `JUDGE-AB` log line, `effort` in the cycle card; a triggered\r\n386:   ladder level overrides it) until 2026-09-28 07:00, and the Monday comparison (minutes, cost, deliveries, judgement\r\n387:   slugs, unchanged-NEXT count) sets the default. External prior: Artificial Analysis index Opus 5.5 medium 51 / high\r\n388:   54 at $1.34 / $1.82 per task.\r\n389: \r\n390: ### INTRA-CYCLE ESCALATION — a card that runs out of budget goes to Fable low, then Fable medium, then to the user (user, 2026-09-25)\r\n391: \r\n392: *\"한 싸이클 내부에서도 특정 프로세스가 과하게 오래 걸리거나 반복적으로 실패할 경우 Fable 5.1 낮음 (그래도 안된다면 Fable\r\n393: 5.1 중간) 높여보는 것이 유효할지\"* — asked while cycle 85 spent 105 min building two missing verbs on Opus. The\r\n394: cycle-level firefighter (fable/low after two failing cycles) already exists; this is the same ladder inside a cycle,\r\n395: per card. Trigger: a `task/1` card returns FAIL with its failure budget spent, or exceeds `budget.minutes`. Rung 1:\r\n396: re-issue the card (`escalation: 1`, `retry_of_card`) to `.claude/agents/material-fable-low.md`; rung 2: `escalation:\r\n397: 2` to `material-fable-medium.md`; then `decisions_pending.json` and stop. Two rungs per card, same flags, rules and\r\n398: peers; the result card carries `escalation`, so Opus-vs-Fable outcomes accumulate as data. Evidence so far: cycles\r\n399: 71/72 (fable/low) delivered L7-1b after two Opus failures. This does not relax the standing rule to minimise Fable —\r\n400: it bounds it to a card that Opus has already failed twice.\r\n401: \r\n402: ### Every cycle ends with a RETROSPECTIVE — the peer loop cannot criticise judgement otherwise (user, 2026-09-15)\r\n403: \r\n404: (User: *\"피어 리뷰를 통해 판단 및 실행 구조에 대한 비평은 할 수 없는 것 같아.\"*) The hypothesis-level reviews all\r\n405: pass and the cycle still goes badly, because a peer only ever sees a framing Claude wrote about a failure Claude\r\n406: chose. Nobody asks whether the question was worth asking, whether twenty failures were one failure, or whether a\r\n407: tool should have come first. So the cycle itself is reviewed, from the machine's record rather than from Claude's\r\n408: summary:\r\n409: \r\n410: 1. `py tools/audit_cycle.py` — mechanical compliance, no judgement: bgrun discipline, every run terminated, every\r\n411:    failing log followed by an archived review, every review annotated, the originals untouched, GUI actions\r\n412:    recorded — plus the cost lines (builds, failures, wall-clock) the retrospective argues about.\r\n413: 2. `py tools/retrospective.py --cycle N` — **v2, ADOPTED 2026-09-16** (v1 frozen at `tools/retrospective_v1.py`,\r\n414:    kept runnable for the comparison only; measurement in `tools/bench/retro_v2_comparison.md`). It dispatches a\r\n415:    FIXED question set (repeated failure · missing tool · inference over measurement · rule compliance · ordering ·\r\n416:    what was not reported · judgement inside a material session) plus a DEVICE EFFECT question built mechanically\r\n417:    from `docs/violation-decisions.md`, with the audit output, the cycle's build logs and CLAUDE.md attached.\r\n418:    Claude does not write the questions and does not choose the evidence. v1 SATURATED — cycles 11/12/13 fired 9/9/6\r\n419:    slugs; v2 fired 2/2/1 with three different top faults.\r\n420: 3. **v2's output contract:** the peer names the **ONE most costly structural fault** (at most two), everything else\r\n421:    is prose FINDINGS with no slug, `VIOLATION: none` is a legitimate and expected answer, and each named fault\r\n422:    carries `loss_min`, `loss_usd` (or `?`) and a clock counterfactual:\r\n423:    `VIOLATION: <slug> | loss_min=<n> | loss_usd=<n or ?> | evidence=<file:line>`. Slugs come from the known list\r\n424:    (`repeated-failure-class`, `tool-not-built`, `inference-over-measurement`, `rule-evaded`, `wrong-ordering`,\r\n425:    `unreported-fact`, `scope-creep`, `premature-build`, `judgement-in-material`, `device-failed` — the last two\r\n426:    added 2026-09-16; `device-failed` has **threshold 1**, because a device that let its own fault through is broken,\r\n427:    not unlucky). `py tools/violations.py` parses both v2's form and v1's bare `VIOLATION: <slug>` and counts them\r\n428:    **across retrospectives, from the files**\r\n429:    — because \"add a device when it repeats\" is worthless if Claude is the one counting (the user's question:\r\n430:    *\"회고가 반복해서 위반을 지적하는지는 어떻게 알아?\"*). **At 3 of the same slug the next cycle must build the\r\n431:    mechanical device for it first.** Only the user may lower that threshold. ✅ **THE 2026-09-18 NO-NEW-DEVICE ORDER IS LIFTED (user, 2026-09-24 03:1x: \"새로운 도구 만드는 걸 내가 막아뒀는데, 보니까 계속 구멍이 생기는 것 같아. 루프 판단에 따라 필요한 도구는 만드는 걸 허용할게\") — after cycle 68 lost ~25 min to two missing scripting verbs (wiring to a loop's `i` terminal; no creator for `Not Equal?`/`Select`) and routed around them. A cycle's JUDGEMENT session decides a tool is necessary (it unblocks the stage, or the same class of work will need it again) and a material session builds it in that cycle, under every existing rule (peer-reviewed plan, stagekit, handle-count hygiene, measured before it acts). Deliverable-first ordering still holds: the tool is built because the deliverable needs it. The threshold below resumes with the same words; the suspension text is kept for history.** ⚠️ **(HISTORY) SUSPENDED since 2026-09-18\r\n432:    08:53** by the user's standing order (*\"장치는 더 민들지 말고 계속 진행\"*, **user, 2026-09-18 08:53** — cited by\r\n433:    DATE, never by a STATUS line number, which every relocation moves): while it\r\n434:    stands, a slug reaching 3 is recorded as a FINDING in `docs/violation-decisions.md` and the next cycle builds\r\n435:    NO device; the threshold resumes the moment the user lifts the order. **EXCEPTION (user, 2026-09-22: \"Jev 건은 예외로 추가하도록 하고\"): devices that put TypeSafe's Jev (a typed-decision model, `docs/jev-integration-plan.md`) behind an EXISTING decision point — the firefighter trigger, the failed-prediction review gate, log classification, plan checks — are allowed under the no-new-device order, each one MEASURED on a labelled set before it is switched on and introduced as an ADVISORY signal first.** Jev scripts (`tools/jev*.py`, `tools/bench/jev_*.py`) are EXEMPT from the failed-prediction and material gates (user, 2026-09-22 \"Jev는 면제\") — they touch no LabVIEW. Thresholds in force: review discharge p≥0.80 (active), firefighter veto p≤0.30 (active), triage/NEXT/prior-art advisory only. **SECOND WAVE APPROVED (user, 2026-09-22 17:3x: \"훌륭하네. 이거 다 적용해보자\") — `docs/jev-integration-plan.md` '2차 후보' 1~7, all of them, including the RULE CHANGE in #1: a failed prediction goes first to the JEV REVIEW LADDER (3-way: our-script-bug / already-reviewed-class / new-problem); only `new-problem` (or no key / unknown band) owes the Opus `hypothesis` review; the other two pass with a logged `JEV-LADDER` line and a citation. Each insertion is measured on a labelled set before it acts; #4 (per-command drift) and #7 (Pre-decided contradictions) are advisory; #6 (5-sample consensus) is the default for every gate call.** **ONE REVIEW PER ROW PER CYCLE (user, 2026-09-22 18:xx \"전부 적용해보자\", after the measured cost: reviews were 30 % of cycle time, 8 in one cycle, mostly a chain of failures on ONE stage row): a failed prediction on the SAME script (basename with `_vN` stripped) that already has an ANSWERED adversary review archived within the last 6 h is DISCHARGED by that review — `guard_peer` cites it (`RULE-SAME-ROW`), no second review is bought. A different script, or a review older than 6 h, still owes its own review. Also: the Jev drift advisory (#4) judges against the WHOLE `## NEXT`, not only its first act; the 10 Pre-decided contradiction suspects from #7 are checked and annotated, not left as a list.** **Second exception (user, 2026-09-22 \"좋아. 다음 사이클에 추가하도록\"): the stage-script library `tools/stagekit.py` (Pre-decided 93) — the repeated skeleton of stage/diagnostic scripts as one verified module, so a stage file is inputs only.** **BUILT 2026-09-22 16:0x (725 lines, self-test 32/0, a 595-line probe re-cut in 120 lines matched 13/13). From now on every new stage or diagnostic is a ≤120-line file on `tools/stagekit.py`; a free-standing multi-hundred-line script is the pattern the user rejected.** Resolved 2026-09-18 by the cycle-26\r\n436:    judgement session after `doc_ingest` reported this line contradicting STATUS — the later user statement wins.\r\n437: 4. `tools/hooks/guard_cycle.py` refuses the next RECIPE build while the previous cycle's logs have no newer\r\n438:    retrospective, or while a slug is at threshold. Diagnostics, docs, peers and the retrospective itself pass.\r\n439: \r\n440: ### When a diagnosis is GUESSED twice, build the reader (2026-09-15)\r\n441: \r\n442: The sibling of \"when a rule is broken twice, move it into a hook\". On 2026-09-15 three consecutive `ExecState 0`\r\n443: failures were diagnosed by inference — branch wire, downcast, transient Remove Bad Wires — each costing a peer review\r\n444: and a rebuild, because the fleet has no way to ask LabVIEW *why* a VI is broken. Each was 3 minutes of measurement\r\n445: dressed as 20 minutes of reasoning. **Rule: the second time a class of failure is explained by inference rather than\r\n446: read from the machine, the next build is the READER for it, not another attempt at the thing that failed.** Readers\r\n447: identified this way: **`Wire.Is Broken?` 6371004 IS BUILT** and measured (`docs/NAMES.md:902-911`, 2026-09-17) — but ONLY inside the connect ops, there is no stand-alone read-only `Wire.Is Broken?` op (STATUS 2026-09-25, `docs/NAMES.md:1081-1089`); use the connect ops' read-back instead of inferring why a wire is bad; `VI.Get Errors` (method 452) is still unbuilt and is probably unreachable over our COM path (absent from the exported `VirtualInstrument` ActiveX interface — `archive/peer/2026-09-18-fstunnel-v1-b4-execstate0-codex.md`), so it is off the critical path. Corrected 2026-09-18 by the cycle-22 judgement session after a `-Dual` review found this line stale.\r\n448: \r\n449: ### Stages are SIMULATED and PRE-RUN OFFLINE before LabVIEW touches them (user, 2026-09-24)\r\n450: \r\n451: *\"미리 사전계획 해서 옮길 vi들 미리 정리하고 만들 struct 미리 계산한 다음 그 결과 어떻게 될지 미리 예측 … Sequential하게\r\n452: 각 단계들을 시뮬레이션 하여 temporary 파일로 저장한 후에 각 사이클의 finalized 플랜을 두는게 맞는듯.\"* Said after the\r\n453: loop-1.7 split failed 6 of 9 LabVIEW runs (≈49 min of LabVIEW, several times that in diagnosis) on problems that were\r\n454: all knowable offline. Eight decisions; design and build order in `docs/stage-simulator-plan.md`:\r\n455: \r\n456: 1. every stage script is DRY-RUN first (COM stubbed); 2. an OFFLINE PRE-RUN (every row decided, every terminal\r\n457: addressable from the graph — unwired ones by owner node → terminal list → uid echo, rows == plan rows);\r\n458: 3. error-cluster and accumulator chains are copied from S1 (`RULE-CHAIN-S1`), never asked of Jev; 4. a failed run\r\n459: invalidates the pre-run records; 5. every cycle starts by reading the bed's LabVIEW Error List through the GUI,\r\n460: each item double-clicked to its location (*\"GUI로 에러 내용 확인하고 각 에러 더블클릭하면 에러 위치로 이동해서\r\n461: 보여주거든\"*); 6. a move's cut set and reconnect table are computed from the graph before the move and checked after;\r\n462: 7. the whole stage is simulated action by action (temporary graph per step, symbolic ids bound by a before/after\r\n463: terminal-table diff) and finalized at `computation_diff` 0, then executed once with a per-step comparison;\r\n464: 8. row content comes ONLY from the finalized plan file — a recipe never re-types a uid or terminal name.\r\n465: **Python computes, Jev picks per-row mechanisms, the LLM designs and takes low-confidence rows.** 1/2/4/7 are\r\n466: enforced by the launch gate in `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py`, 5 by `cycle_runner.py`'s `errorlist_hook`, 3/6/7/8 by\r\n467: stagekit gates; this section records only the why.\r\n468: \r\n469: ### Big or blocked work is SPLIT into steps that each SAVE an intermediate artefact (user, 2026-09-19)\r\n470: \r\n471: *\"앞으로도 프로젝트 방향성에 관해서 큰 덩어리의 프로젝트는, 혹은 병목이 생긴 부분에서는, 세부적으로 쪼개서 중간과정\r\n472: 저장하며 진행하는 방식으로 규칙 수정 필요할듯.\"* — said after ten overnight route-B attempts (v3→v7, 5 cycles, ≈$166)\r\n473: edited a VI copy entirely in LabVIEW memory, died at the same re-wiring stage every time, and left **no file to look\r\n474: at** (the \"preserved\" crash copies were byte-identical to the untouched original). Applies to everything still ahead\r\n475: (EMCCD connection/sync, display, …), not only D1.\r\n476: \r\n477: 1. **A step is not done until it has left a file.** Every build stage saves its intermediate VI/data under `claudeDev`,\r\n478:    records its md5, and the next stage starts FROM THAT FILE — in a fresh LabVIEW instance when the stage uses VI\r\n479:    Scripting. The user must always have something to open.\r\n480: 2. **Default granularity = one saved artefact per natural stage** (copy → structures → moves → re-wiring in batches of\r\n481:    10–15 rows → census → final save). Not one script per wire (*\"일일히 배선 하나하나 별도 스크립트를 쓰는 건 낭비\"*).\r\n482: 3. **Re-splitting is triggered, not felt** (user: the decision is the judgement session's, the trigger is the\r\n483:    machine's): the same stage failing twice at the same place, or a stage that ends without a saved artefact ⇒ the\r\n484:    next cycle's FIRST act is a **decomposition plan** for that stage (one page: sub-steps, each's saved file name and\r\n485:    pass criterion), prior-art-reviewed once, then executed step by step by material sessions. A full-length retry under\r\n486:    a new file name (`_v8`) is forbidden. `cycle_runner.py` counts renamed recipes as the same recipe.\r\n487: 4. A new large stage (e.g. EMCCD sync) starts with its step list and saved-file list written into the plan; no build\r\n488:    before that list exists.\r\n489: 6. **A BROKEN INTERMEDIATE MAY BE SAVED BY GUI Ctrl+S (user, 2026-09-22: \"저장 허용함.\").** A `claudeDev` stage artefact that is broken BY DESIGN (its missing rows belong to the next stage, `ExecState` 0) is saved through `gui_save` (block-diagram window fronted and click-probed, Ctrl+S, mtime verified) with `-Exception Approved -Evidence \"user 2026-09-22 broken-intermediate save\"`, because COM `SaveInstrument` hangs on a broken VI — the scripted route is verified unreachable for this class. Such a file is NEVER run and never cold-loaded headless. Not for originals (rule 1) and not for the final deliverable, which must reach `ExecState` 1 and save by script.\r\n490: 5. **Reference hygiene is a precondition, not an afterthought**: an op that traverses without `Close Reference` is\r\n491:    repaired before it is used in a staged build (20 consecutive calls in one script, handle count flat ±100).\r\n492: \r\n493: ### The work cycle (user, 2026-08-31)\r\n494: \r\n495: 1) **Plan, and peer-review the plan**; 2) write the WHOLE build as **one long script file**\r\n496: (Python/bash — e.g. `tools/recipes/*.py`), every name resolved from `docs/NAMES.md` at planning\r\n497: time; 3) execute the file as a batch; 4) read the results report, revise; 5) repeat. No mid-run\r\n498: name discovery; no per-step verification round-trips. Target: replay speed, discovery paid up\r\n499: front.\r\n500: \r\n501: ### Tooling\r\n502: \r\n503: **Hooks (mechanical rules, 2026-09-04)** in `.claude/settings.json`: `tools/hooks/answer_first.py`\r\n504: (UserPromptSubmit) re-injects rule 2b on every user message; `tools/hooks/guard_bash.py`\r\n505: (PreToolUse Bash|PowerShell) blocks any LabVIEW-touching command run in the foreground without a\r\n506: timeout ≤ 30 s — background it instead. Prose rules fade after compaction; code rules do not\r\n507: (the GUI gate in lv_gui.ps1 ended six rounds of GUI-creep). When a rule is broken twice, move it\r\n508: into a hook. Hook commands use forward-slash paths (backslashes are escape-interpreted). **Project hooks also run\r\n509: inside every `claude -p` cell spawned from this directory** — a hook that gives advice (\"stalled client,\r\n510: stop the task\") will be acted on by that cell; gate such hooks on an env var the spawner sets\r\n511: (`BENCH_CELL`), as lv_stallcheck.ps1 now does.\r\n512: \r\n513: **Patch files with the Edit/Write tools, never with a `py - <<'EOF'` heredoc** (2026-09-09: three heredoc patches\r\n514: in one session died on `\\U`/`\\N` escapes or literal NUL bytes, and because the launch line followed on a new line\r\n515: the chain ran UNPATCHED each time — 20 min lost per round). A heredoc is fine for read-only one-liners only.\r\n516: \r\n517: All screenshot/window/mouse/keyboard/checksum operations go through\r\n518: [tools/lv_gui.ps1](tools/lv_gui.ps1) — never inline `Add-Type`. `.claude/settings.json`\r\n519: pre-approves it **only** in the exact form `& .\\tools\\lv_gui.ps1 -Action ...` from the project root\r\n520: — a `cd`, a variable, or an absolute path breaks the allowlist and stalls unattended work. The same\r\n521: settings **deny** Edit/Write on `*.vi`, `*.ctl`, `*.lvlib`, `*.lvproj` (compiled binaries; every\r\n522: legitimate change happens inside LabVIEW) — defense-in-depth for rule 1.\r\n523: \r\n524: ## 4. Documentation is layered: short top index, topic files below, archive unread\r\n525: \r\n526: (User, 2026-08-27 + 2026-08-31 \"계층화\".) **Active docs** state what is true now, in as little text\r\n527: as a cold start needs: `STATUS.md` stays under one screen and only points; verified facts go to\r\n528: topic files (`docs/`, skill `references/`); narrative goes to [archive/](archive/) — nothing is\r\n529: deleted, only moved. **Do not read `archive/` in normal work**; open a specific file only when the\r\n530: active docs are ambiguous, and say why. When any working doc needs scrolling to find the current\r\n531: state, push content down a layer immediately, not at the next cleanup. Scratch artefacts\r\n532: (throwaway VIs, build targets) are created and deleted in the same operation.\r\n533: \r\n534: **Concrete threshold (2026-09-15, after STATUS.md reached 651 lines):** STATUS.md over ~100 lines means the cycle\r\n535: narrative has crept back in — move it to `archive/<date>-status-<topic>.md` and leave lock, hardware permission,\r\n536: current state, OPEN items and NEXT. The narrative is never rewritten, only relocated.\r\n537: \r\n538: ### Documents are LINTED by code and INGESTED by a model every cycle (user, 2026-09-16)\r\n539: \r\n540: *\"특정 주기마다 .md 파일들 ingest 및 lint 하는 규약 필요해보임\"* — after STATUS reached 526 lines, CLAUDE.md carried a\r\n541: stale slug list and two docs described one wire two ways. Cadence is **per cycle / per run, never weekly**\r\n542: (*\"주 단위보다는 싸이클 단위 혹은 실제 실행 단위가 적절해보임\"*). \"Ingest\" means **a model reads and refreshes\r\n543: state** — NOT an index; the index was measured and rejected (`tools/bench/priorart_scores.md`).\r\n544: \r\n545: | layer | what | model | when |\r\n546: |---|---|---|---|\r\n547: | **lint** | `tools/doc_lint.py` — frontmatter valid; every cited path / `file:line` exists; STATUS ≤ ~100 lines; one `status: current` plan; `supersedes:` targets not still current; review dispositions not placeholders; unmarked decision sentences (warn) | none (a `.py`) | every cycle close, run by `audit_cycle` |\r\n548: | **ingest — changed docs** | `tools/doc_ingest.py --cycle N` — the claude peer as rule/consistency auditor (`peer.ps1 -Role ingest`, archived to `archive/ingest/`, invisible to every gate) over the cycle's changed files (audit C7 list): contradictions between them and with the active docs | **Sonnet** | every cycle close |\r\n549: | **ingest — all active docs** | `tools/doc_ingest.py --full --model opus` — same audit over all of `docs/` + STATUS + CLAUDE.md | **Opus** | every 5 cycles (same rhythm as the outcome review) |\r\n550: | **resolving** which document is right | — | judgement session | when the ingest reports a contradiction |\r\n551: \r\n552: ## 5. Peer agents (Codex, Gemini) — Claude manages, they only advise\r\n553: \r\n554: Read-only research sub-agents; their brief is [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). **Dispatch only through\r\n555: [`tools/peer.ps1`](tools/peer.ps1)** (`-Agent codex|gemini|claude [-Kind review|fact] -Slug <name>\r\n556: -Task \"...\"` — nothing else validates). It enforces read-only, bounds every call with a timeout, classifies the outcome, and\r\n557: archives the exchange. Screenshots and reporter text may be attached; **never `.vi` files**. Attach\r\n558: a ~3-line \"already ruled out\" block for diagnostic questions; none for pure API facts. **A peer\r\n559: answer is a hypothesis** — confirm against the machine or the vendor's own files; two models\r\n560: agreeing is not confirmation. Call them *before building something expensive* and whenever a\r\n561: diagnosis is about to drive real work; dispatch needs no approval and has no call limit. On quota\r\n562: exhaustion stop calling that agent for the session — degrade to working without it, never to\r\n563: waiting.\r\n564: \r\n565: ### Review has THREE layers, and only the third asks whether the work was worth doing (2026-09-15)\r\n566: \r\n567: | layer | asks | fires | enforced by |\r\n568: |---|---|---|---|\r\n569: | hypothesis | is this diagnosis right? | every failed prediction | `guard_peer.py` |\r\n570: | cycle | was this cycle run well? | end of every cycle | `guard_cycle.py` + `retrospective.py` |\r\n571: | **outcome** | **did any of it move the deliverable?** | **every 5 cycles or 7 days** | `guard_cycle.py` + `outcome_review.py` |\r\n572: \r\n573: The first two both ask HOW; a cycle can be run impeccably and still be the wrong cycle (user,\r\n574: 2026-09-15: \"주기적으로 프로젝트의 성과를 판단하는 피어도 필요하지 않을까\"). The outcome layer asks\r\n575: against `project-requirements/`: what can the user RUN today that they could not before; which\r\n576: numbered requirement has not moved since the project began; is the cost-to-product ratio defensible;\r\n577: if the user had to run an experiment next week, would they use the original VI or anything we built.\r\n578: Its reviewer was **codex, never claude** — \"was this worth doing\" is the question a reviewer sharing our priors is\r\n579: worst at. ⚠️ **CHANGED 2026-09-18, a TRIAL (see \"Codex's roles move to Claude sub-sessions\" below): the reviewer is\r\n580: `peer.ps1 -Agent claude -Role outcome` = FABLE / medium, run `--safe-mode` so the cell never loads CLAUDE.md,\r\n581: STATUS.md's framing or the plan documents into its system prompt.** The original objection is answered by the THIN\r\n582: PROMPT rather than by the vendor — what made codex right here was not sharing our priors, and a cell that reads\r\n583: only `project-requirements/` and the counted output does not share them either. `-Agent codex -Kind fact` is one\r\n584: flag away if the trial fails. **Fired three times (2026-09-15, 2026-09-16, 2026-09-18: zero runnable experimental VIs every time); the\r\n585: user re-planned to DELIVERY-FIRST on 2026-09-16 (\"A로 진행하자\") — `docs/cycle15-plan.md` — with the boundary\r\n586: *\"여기는 중간 과정일 뿐 결국에는 최종 스텝으로 나가야함\"*: delivery is ordering, the seven-loop restructure is\r\n587: still the goal.** **An `OUTCOME-VIOLATION` is NOT answered by building a device** (that is the\r\n588: process rule, and answering goal drift with another tool is how the drift happened): the next cycle\r\n589: becomes a **delivery** cycle, and on repetition the work stops for a re-plan with the user. **AMENDED by the user 2026-09-23 (\"83번으로 가자\", resolving Pre-decided 44 vs 83): once the user has ANSWERED a repeated outcome verdict (as on 2026-09-20, \"계속\"), a further repetition of the SAME verdict does NOT stop the runner — the session marks the escalation in STATUS and the interactive chat reports it; the work stops only when the verdict's CONTENT changes (a new violation line, or a requirement moving backwards).** Keep the\r\n590: layer to one script, one dispatch and one gate line — the user's explicit budget for it. **STEERING CARD (user, 2026-09-24: \"아웃컴 리뷰에 조향카드 부여하는 것 동의\"):** a repeated outcome verdict now also emits a `steer/1` card (the next cycle's required act, tied to `docs/goalmap.json` ids); the judgement session FOLLOWS it or REFUSES it with cited evidence in `next.json`; two refusals of the same item stop the runner and put it on `tools/bench/decisions_pending.json`. **RETRY CAP (user, same day: \"재시도 상한도 동의함. 다만 숫자 … 데이터를 쌓아가면서 조정\"):** one stage's LabVIEW runs per cycle are capped (`tools/stage_prerun.py` `RETRY_CAP`, start 2); a further run needs a judgement card; per-stage run counts are recorded so the number is tuned from data.\r\n591: \r\n592: **The fourth layer — PRIOR ART (\"has this already been done here?\", `prior_art_review.py`, gated by the same\r\n593: `guard_cycle.py`) — stops the work on any verdict other than `novel`, and there are exactly TWO releases, each\r\n594: written into the review file itself and each paid for with a citation:**\r\n595: \r\n596: | release | means | conditions (all machine-checked) |\r\n597: |---|---|---|\r\n598: | `REFUTED: <slug> - <file>:<line> says X, which does not cover Y because …` | the review is **wrong** | open the cited file and show in writing that it does not cover this case. Not \"I think otherwise\" |\r\n599: | `FIXED: <slug> - <path>:<line> - <one sentence saying what changed>` | the review is **right and the work already changed** | (a) `<path>` exists under the project, (b) it was last changed **after** the review (its frontmatter date; the file's own stamp if it carries none), (c) the line sits under `## What was done with it` in the review file |\r\n600: \r\n601: `FIXED:` was added 2026-09-16 (tested: valid releases; nonexistent path does not; older-than-the-review path does\r\n602: not). Until then the gate's own refusal said \"if the review is right, change the plan instead\" while accepting only\r\n603: the branch where the review is WRONG — so a round whose findings were all correct and all acted on could not\r\n604: release the build it had just improved. An assertion is not a refutation and **a promise is not a fix**; the\r\n605: citation is the currency, and `CYCLE_GUARD_OFF` is never the answer.\r\n606: \r\n607: **The peer must be asked to REFUTE — now mechanical.** `peer.ps1 -Kind review` (the default) REFUSES\r\n608: a task carrying confirm-bait (\"please confirm\", \"sanity check\", \"do you agree\", \"확인 부탁\") and\r\n609: APPENDS the adversarial instruction set (strongest reason it is wrong · an alternative explanation ·\r\n610: what would falsify it · the cheapest discriminating test). `-Kind fact` exempts a pure API-fact\r\n611: question. Cycle 7's retrospective found prompts saying \"BRIEF CONFIRM\"; a rule about how a question\r\n612: is *phrased* belongs in the dispatcher, not in good intentions.\r\n613: \r\n614: **The claude peer cannot discharge a failed prediction.** It is the rule/consistency auditor, so\r\n615: `guard_peer.py` requires the lifting exchange to come from codex or gemini **and** to have ended\r\n616: `ANSWERED` — a TIMEOUT/QUOTA/ERROR exchange is exactly what this file calls \"told you nothing\", and\r\n617: the gate used to accept one.\r\n618: \r\n619: ### External search is MANDATORY — the ladder only decides who runs it\r\n620: \r\n621: (User, three times, finally: \"멀티 에이전트 활용을 하던 아니면 너 혼자 답변을 하던 외부 검색 세션을\r\n622: 필수로 넣도록\".) Every factual question about a tool, API, error or capability gets an external\r\n623: search — by a peer or by Claude, never skipped. The error being stamped out is **false confidence\r\n624: from local evidence**: absence in what you happen to be looking at is not evidence of absence.\r\n625: **Trigger sentence:** the moment you are about to write \"X is not possible\", \"our tools cannot\r\n626: reach that\", or \"so we fall back to GUI clicking\" — stop and search. Claims about *our own tools*\r\n627: are factual claims, the ones you are most likely to be wrong about.\r\n628: \r\n629: ### A FAILED PREDICTION triggers mandatory peer review — all three parts\r\n630: \r\n631: (User, 2026-08-30: \"가설을 세워서 결과와 맞지 않으면 피어리뷰 과정을 필수로 넣자. 내가 말한 피어리뷰는\r\n632: 멀티에이전트 검색 및 감시, 더해서 advocate 과정이야\".) **A recurring error alert is a failed prediction too**\r\n633: (user, 2026-09-14, on the repeated \"STALLED LabVIEW client\" hook messages: \"이런 에러들도 반복되는 것 같으니\r\n634: 피어리뷰 반드시 필요하겠어. 규율에 적용하도록\") — mechanically: `tools/lv_stallcheck.ps1` writes\r\n635: `tools/bench/stall_pid<N>.log` (`STALL:` line) and `tools/hooks/guard_peer.py` blocks the next build until\r\n636: a peer review newer than that record is archived. When any other error class shows up a second time, give\r\n637: it the same treatment in the same session: a record the gate can see, not a sentence here. **The gate reads build\r\n638: logs only — `tools/bench/peer_*.log` transcripts are excluded** (2026-09-15: a reviewer's own sentence, \"Fail the\r\n639: build on inequality…\", blocked the very build that review had approved; review logs are evidence, never the thing\r\n640: under test). The moment you predicted X and observed\r\n641: not-X, the explanation you form is a fresh hypothesis built under pressure. Peer review then means\r\n642: **all three together**: (1) multi-agent search asked to *attack* the claim; (2) **monitoring** —\r\n643: watch the dispatch and classify its outcome; a call that failed, timed out or hit quota told you\r\n644: *nothing* (an `-Agent agy` ValidateSet typo once died instantly and would have passed for a\r\n645: completed review); (3) the devil's-advocate pass below. **Your own successful discriminating test\r\n646: does not discharge this** — it confirms the experiment you thought to run; the peer's job is to\r\n647: attack the framing.\r\n648: \r\n649: ### The devil's-advocate pass — search catches ignorance; this catches false knowledge\r\n650: \r\n651: (User, 2026-08-29: \"외부 검색만이 아니라 자체적으로 devil's advocate이 필요하겠는데\".) Before a\r\n652: diagnosis or plan drives real work — and at every \"X is impossible\", before expensive construction,\r\n653: before destructive steps — argue against it. Two requirements keep it from being theatre: **it must\r\n654: end in a discriminating test** (name an alternative cause, name what would falsify the hypothesis,\r\n655: run the cheapest separator — comparing options is not a test); and **a peer used as adversary must\r\n656: be asked to refute, not confirm**. Health check: if the user is the one raising the objections, the\r\n657: internal pass is not running.\r\n658: \r\n659: ### Name the level of verification — structural is not functional\r\n660: \r\n661: (User, 2026-08-29: \"데이터 넣어보지 않았는데 어떻게 확인한 거야?\") **Structural** = the artefact is\r\n662: well-formed and legal (object counts, owners, `ExecState == 1` — the compiler's verdict, nothing\r\n663: more). **Functional** = the right numbers when real data flows through. A build that has never been\r\n664: run is not verified, however many counts agree. State the level explicitly in STATUS and in every\r\n665: summary.\r\n666: \r\n667: ### The research ladder — one rung at a time, and it always has a bottom\r\n668: \r\n669: | | reads files | reads the web | model |\r\n670: |---|---|---|---|\r\n671: | **codex** | ✅ project dir, read-only sandbox | ✅ | `gpt-5.6-sol` / medium, pinned in peer.ps1 (user, 2026-09-15). **Still selectable, no longer any default — weekly quota 9 % on 2026-09-18** |\r\n672: | **agy (gemini)** | ❌ | ✅ | RETIRED from every default/fallback 2026-09-22 (user: roles delegated to claude; headless permission auto-deny). Explicit `-Agent gemini` only |\r\n673: | **claude** | ✅ project dir (the thin roles: only what they choose to read), plan mode + acting tools denied | per role | **`-Role` decides**: `audit` sonnet · `ingest` sonnet · `priorart` claude-opus-5-5/medium · `hypothesis` claude-opus-5-5/high +web (user table 2026-09-23; was opus/high · opus/max) · `fact` **fable/low +web, thin** · `outcome` **fable/medium +web, thin** · `prose` **fable/low, thin** |\r\n674: \r\n675: The claude peer was added 2026-09-15 on the user's direction (\"claude 하위 세션도 peer review에 참여\r\n676: 시키는게 좋겠어\") so the review structure survives an external quota outage. It is the one peer that\r\n677: spends the SAME subscription as the main session. **THIN** = dispatched with `--safe-mode`, so the cell skips\r\n678: CLAUDE.md, skills, plugins, hooks and MCP; that fixed context load, not the model, was the ~$1.5–3 a peer call\r\n679: cost. Web is now per role, not banned outright — `hypothesis`, `fact` and `outcome` have `WebSearch`/`WebFetch`;\r\n680: `audit`, `ingest`, `priorart` and `prose` still have none.\r\n681: \r\n682: One peer per question (the others only to cross-check an answer about to drive expensive construction).\r\n683: Fallback order from 2026-09-22 (user: \"Gemini 역할도 claude에게 위임\" — agy auto-denies its `command` permission headless, so it is dead in unattended runs): **claude → Claude's own research session**; gemini stays selectable by an explicit `-Agent gemini` only, with codex reachable by an\r\n684: explicit `-Agent codex` while its quota lasts. If every peer is exhausted, Claude still runs the external research\r\n685: itself — \"no peer available\" never means going straight to experimenting. Waiting for a quota to renew is never\r\n686: the right move.\r\n687: \r\n688: ### Codex's roles move to Claude sub-sessions — TRIAL, user's decision 2026-09-18\r\n689: \r\n690: > *\"Codex 잔여량이 생각보다 얼마 남지 않음. 주간 한도 9% 남았음. 아무래도 Codex가 수행중인 역할을 fable로\r\n691: > 구동하는게 어떨까 싶음.\"* … *\"우선은 지금 말한 방법으로 몇 번 돌려보자\"*\r\n692: \r\n693: A trial, not a settled rule: run it a few times, then decide. What changed, all inside `peer.ps1`'s role table:\r\n694: \r\n695: | dispatch | before | from 2026-09-18 |\r\n696: |---|---|---|\r\n697: | `-Kind fact`, no `-Agent` | codex by hand | **claude `-Role fact`** — fable/low, thin, web |\r\n698: | `-Kind prose`, no `-Agent` | codex (`reporter`) | **claude `-Role prose`** — fable/low, thin |\r\n699: | `outcome_review.py` | `-Agent codex` | **claude `-Role outcome`** — fable/medium, thin, web |\r\n700: | failed prediction | `-Dual` (codex **and** opus/max) | **`-Agent claude -Role hypothesis` SINGLE arm**; `-Dual` stays available |\r\n701: | `prior_art_review.py`, `doc_ingest.py`, `retrospective.py` | unchanged | unchanged |\r\n702: \r\n703: **D3 is amended**: `guard_peer.py` now accepts an ANSWERED `claude` exchange whose `role:` is `hypothesis`\r\n704: (opus / effort max) as discharging a failed prediction, alongside codex and gemini. Every other claude role still\r\n705: cannot — it is the rule/consistency audit, not a framing adversary. Check a routing change without spending a\r\n706: call: `tools/peer.ps1 -Kind fact -Slug x -Task \"...\" -DryRun` prints agent, role, model, effort, tools and the\r\n707: archive path and dispatches nothing.\r\n708: \r\n709: ### Hypothesis reviews are being moved to an Opus/max Claude peer — measured first (user, 2026-09-17)\r\n710: \r\n711: GPT usage was higher than the user expected (*\"GPT 사용량이 생각보다 적지 않은데\"*). Decision: try the failed-\r\n712: prediction (hypothesis) review on a Claude sub-session under **codex's exact constraints** (read-only, adversarial\r\n713: preamble, timeout, archive, gates) with **Opus at effort MAX** (*\"opus는 high 보다 더 높게 잡아도 문제 없을 것\r\n714: 같은데\"*) and **web search enabled** for that role. Not Fable (cost rule). codex stays for the OUTCOME review and\r\n715: as the second opinion when a Claude review passes a claim about our own tools. **The next five failed predictions\r\n716: go to BOTH; D3 (\"a claude peer cannot discharge a failed prediction\") is amended only after that comparison.**\r\n717: Implementation: `peer.ps1` role `hypothesis` (opus/max, WebSearch allowed) + a dual-dispatch switch; the archive's\r\n718: cost lines are the comparison data.\r\n719: \r\n720: ✅ **BUILT AND SELF-TESTED 2026-09-17** (`tools/bench/peer_dual_selftest.log`, `BGRUN END rc=0 after 100s`):\r\n721: `tools/peer.ps1 -Agent claude -Role hypothesis` = **opus / effort max** with `--allowedTools WebSearch WebFetch\r\n722: Read Glob Grep` (the only claude role with the web; every other one keeps rule 5's \"claude peer has no web\"), and\r\n723: **`-Dual`** re-invokes the script twice on one `-TaskFile`, archiving `<date>-<slug>-codex.md` and\r\n724: `<date>-<slug>-opus.md`. Both arms ANSWERED on the self-test; the cost lines are\r\n725: `codex 26 s` (no cost line — the CLI reports none) vs **`opus $1.8621, in 10 / out 4,581 / cache-create 158,866 /\r\n726: cache-read 258,782, 70 s, 11 turns`**. ⚠️ `-Dual` exits with **codex's** return code.\r\n727: ✅ **SUPERSEDED 2026-09-18 by the trial above**: the comparison stopped early because codex's weekly quota reached\r\n728: 9 %. **D3 IS AMENDED** — `guard_peer.py` accepts `-Agent claude -Role hypothesis` (ANSWERED, opus/max) on its own,\r\n729: so a failed-prediction review is now a **SINGLE claude arm**, not `-Dual`. `-Dual` remains available and is the\r\n730: right call when a claim about our OWN tools needs a second opinion that does not share our priors.\r\n731: \r\n732: ### Archiving peer exchanges — and the one exception to rule 4\r\n733: \r\n734: Every exchange goes to `archive/peer/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` (question, answer, source URLs);\r\n735: conclusions that changed direction get copied into an active doc. **Exception:** check\r\n736: `archive/peer/` for the same question before re-asking — re-asking wastes quota the session may\r\n737: need later.\r\n738: \r\n739: ## Where to actually look\r\n740: \r\n741: - [STATUS.md](STATUS.md) — lock, current state, next actions. Read first, always. One screen.\r\n742: - [docs/NAMES.md](docs/NAMES.md) — verified terminal/label strings (newlines are real). Check\r\n743:   before every wiring call.\r\n744: - `.claude/skills/labview-automation/` — ALL LabVIEW technique (SKILL.md index + 4 references).\r\n745:   Invoke the skill before any LabVIEW work.\r\n746: - [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md) — the rig. [Requests.md](Requests.md) — the user's ground\r\n747:   rules verbatim. [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) — the peers' brief; keep it short and true.\r\n748: - [archive/](archive/) — history; see rule 4.\r\nFILE: tools/bench/cards/task_90-6.json\r\n1: {\r\n2:  \"schema\": \"task/1\",\r\n3:  \"id\": \"90-6\",\r\n4:  \"kind\": \"build\",\r\n5:  \"goal\": \"Retry of 90-5 per PD197(g): claudeDev\\\\D1_s1_t0_<ts>.vi with stamps on While-body and For-body sites, ExecState read after every site\",\r\n6:  \"why\": \"90-5 FAIL, budget spent: run 1 ExecState 0, cause unmeasured (junk Invokes / bare site-0 CLFN / a site refusing). PD197(g) fixes the route and makes the per-site ExecState read the measurement.\",\r\n7:  \"inputs\": [\r\n8:   {\"path\": \"tools/bench/cards/result_90-5.json\"},\r\n9:   {\"path\": \"docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md\"},\r\n10:   {\"path\": \"archive/peer/2026-09-26-c90-t0step3-movewire.md\"},\r\n11:   {\"path\": \"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.py\", \"md5\": \"24da7f2de327cda39fc84b38615573a2\"},\r\n12:   {\"path\": \"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.log\"},\r\n13:   {\"path\": \"tools/bench/t0_sites_s1.json\"},\r\n14:   {\"path\": \"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_smoke.py\"},\r\n15:   {\"path\": \"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\National Instruments\\\\LabVIEW 2026\\\\user.lib\\\\claudeDev\\\\D1_s1_copy.vi\", \"md5\": \"3e3d23cefd3a334001aa9d6156bf1aee\"}\r\n16:  ],\r\n17:  \"pass\": [\r\n18:   \"sites: 0,2,8 (#637 body), 3,4,5 (For bodies in #637), 10-13 (#15266), 16,17 (For #16621 body), 20 (#25392); case sites 6/7/14/15 not attempted\",\r\n19:   \"per site: move_in, junk Invoke purged, const on t6 via OpCreateConstOnTerm_v0, branch Is Broken? False + sink +1, then ExecState READ and logged\",\r\n20:   \"table site -> ExecState after it; the first site that turns ExecState 0 (if any) named with its wire type and the node's error text if readable\",\r\n21:   \"if final ExecState 1: computation_diff(S1,new) rows 0, added = CLFN + constants only (no Invoke); saved by script as D1_s1_t0_<ts>.vi, md5\",\r\n22:   \"if saved: smoke run (diag_c90_t0_smoke.py, <=60 s, fresh T0STAMP_DIR) -> per-site file counts; else report why not run\",\r\n23:   \"D1_s1_copy, kswap, L2-A1 bed md5 unchanged; LabVIEW verified gone; TMX 39 after any real run\"\r\n24:  ],\r\n25:  \"outputs\": [\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\National Instruments\\\\LabVIEW 2026\\\\user.lib\\\\claudeDev\\\\D1_s1_t0_*.vi\", \"tools/bench/t0_sites_s1_step3*\", \"tools/bench/diag_c90_*\", \"tools/bench/cards/result_90-6.json\"],\r\n26:  \"flags\": {\r\n27:   \"labview\": \"build\",\r\n28:   \"gui\": true,\r\n29:   \"hardware\": \"gate\",\r\n30:   \"run_vi\": true,\r\n31:   \"write\": [\r\n32:    \"C:/Program Files/National Instruments/LabVIEW 2026/user.lib/claudeDev/D1_s1_t0_*\",\r\n33:    \"C:/Program Files/National Instruments/LabVIEW 2026/user.lib/claudeDev/scratch_c90_*\",\r\n34:    \"tools/bench/t0_sites_s1_step3*\",\r\n35:    \"tools/bench/t0_legs/**\",\r\n36:    \"tools/bench/diag_c90_*\",\r\n37:    \"tools/bench/stage_runs.jsonl\",\r\n38:    \"tools/bench/jev_gate.log\",\r\n39:    \"tools/bench/jev_usage.jsonl\",\r\n40:    \"tools/bench/errorlist_shots/**\",\r\n41:    \"tools/bench/gui_shots/**\",\r\n42:    \"tools/gui_actions.log\",\r\n43:    \"tools/bench/peer_*\",\r\n44:    \"tools/bench/cards/**\",\r\n45:    \"archive/peer/**\"\r\n46:   ],\r\n47:   \"status_edit\": false,\r\n48:   \"git_commit\": false,\r\n49:   \"peers\": [\"hypothesis\"]\r\n50:  },\r\n51:  \"budget\": {\"failures\": 2, \"minutes\": 50},\r\n52:  \"rules\": [\r\n53:   \"escalation 2 of card 90-5 (material runs fable/low until 2026-09-28); same rules as 90-5\",\r\n54:   \"input is a BYTE COPY of D1_s1_copy.vi; never open-for-edit D1_s1_copy, kswap, the bed or originals\",\r\n55:   \"build step before smoke; a saved ExecState-1 file is the step-3 artefact even if the smoke is not reached\",\r\n56:   \"GUI only for bead picking in the smoke (capture->act->confirm, Exception Approved)\",\r\n57:   \"hold your turn until every bgrun you start has its END/TIMEOUT line; LabVIEW gone before return\",\r\n58:   \"facts only; result/1 <=10 facts <=3 open\"\r\n59:  ],\r\n60:  \"escalation\": 2,\r\n61:  \"retry_of_card\": \"90-5\",\r\n62:  \"advances\": [\"M8\", \"M3\", \"R1\", \"R3\"]\r\n63: }\r\nFILE: tools/bench/cards/result_90-5.json\r\n1: {\"schema\":\"result/1\",\"id\":\"90-5\",\"status\":\"FAIL\",\"gates\":{\"pass\":33,\"fail\":10},\r\n2:  \"first_fail\":\"diag_c90_t0_step3.log:30 S00.3 CLFN on Diagram[43]=#639 N[73], t6 wire 0 after moving CLFN+constant (tunnels unchanged)\",\r\n3:  \"blocked_by\":{\"device\":\"tools/hooks/guard_peer.py + budget\",\"message\":\"run 2 launch (04:40) refused until the run-1 failed prediction had its hypothesis review (JEV-LADDER new-problem p=0.712, NEXT-ACTION old path); review ANSWERED 04:47 (312 s); 63 of 70 budget minutes then spent, no second LabVIEW run launched\"},\r\n4:  \"artefacts\":[\r\n5:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.py\",\"md5\":\"24da7f2de327cda39fc84b38615573a2\"},\r\n6:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.log\",\"md5\":\"ac019930ab9b146d2970be3765a0afa9\"},\r\n7:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/t0_sites_s1_step3.json\",\"md5\":\"c4e5c44b769c87eb023fd435da8d20a2\"},\r\n8:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3_dry.log\",\"md5\":\"ea112a05ded944c2ff7950416f652041\"},\r\n9:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_smoke.py\",\"md5\":\"45dd2755d44d3301633240dfca3630e5\"},\r\n10:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_wikiq.py\",\"md5\":\"6eab82f8f7d9de7dd787226700968d2f\"},\r\n11:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_wikiq.log\",\"md5\":\"26520c8ac32f595c34ad79f47d4b907c\"},\r\n12:   {\"path\":\"archive/peer/2026-09-26-c90-t0step3-movewire.md\",\"md5\":\"d3e7d835b7270bd3a47dc271bba6765c\"},\r\n13:   {\"path\":\"tools/bench/peer_c90_t0step3_movewire.log\",\"md5\":\"439cb718e0a67e566a2c9692b693cf36\"}\r\n14:  ],\r\n15:  \"facts\":[\r\n16:   \"NO D1_s1_t0 file delivered: run 1 ended ExecState 0 (diag_c90_t0_step3.log:195-199), not saved, work copy deleted (:207); pins/input unchanged (:208-216); LabVIEW gone (:227), tasklist empty 04:49\",\r\n17:   \"Site 1 OMITTED per card pass 1: no grab node in loop #637 - IMAQdx Grab #15403 is on display body #15266, IMAQ